-- Book 1 · Lesson 2.2: General Revelation: Creation Testifies to God
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('0cd1e41a-0d02-56c5-bfd9-824353316601', 'ec498105-96de-575e-970e-ff6ee92f97fd', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does this passage’s specific declaration — creation itself “declaring” God’s glory — reveal that every person, even before hearing the gospel explicitly, has genuine access to some knowledge of God through what has been made?

This passage’s specific declaration reveals genuine access to knowledge of God through creation, since creation “declaring” His glory establishes this available testimony, meaning every person, even before explicit gospel proclamation, has this genuine, though limited, access to knowledge of God’s existence and glory, but this passage’s specific declaration particularly requiring recognition of this general revelation as genuine, though ultimately insufficient for salvation, testimony available throughout all creation and humanity.$gs$),
  ('fac6c1f7-32b2-5d7d-9225-aac80065ed46', 'ec498105-96de-575e-970e-ff6ee92f97fd', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does recognizing this general revelation explain both humanity’s genuine accountability before God — since this testimony leaves no one entirely without knowledge — and the ongoing necessity of the specific, gospel message that general revelation alone cannot provide?

Recognizing general revelation explains both genuine accountability and gospel necessity, since accountability without excuse and gospel necessity coexist, meaning humanity remains genuinely accountable given this available testimony, while still genuinely needing the specific gospel message this general revelation alone cannot provide, following this passage’s specific declaration that establishes both truths together throughout believers’ understanding of humanity’s condition and need.$gs$),
  ('2e2da87f-bf6a-5c09-b666-fcbc04952b40', 'ec498105-96de-575e-970e-ff6ee92f97fd', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Affirming general revelation does not mean creation alone is sufficient for salvation; it means people are genuinely without excuse for unbelief while still genuinely needing the specific, saving message of the gospel that only special revelation — Scripture and gospel proclamation — can provide.$gs$),
  ('cf533c2c-c50d-5d66-8b72-f11d821d0b96', 'ec498105-96de-575e-970e-ff6ee92f97fd', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read Psalm 19:1-4 and Romans 1:19-20 and reflect on creation’s genuine testimony to God.
2. Spend time outdoors this week intentionally reflecting on creation’s testimony to God’s glory.
3. Write about how you might use this testimony as a natural starting point in a gospel conversation.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how general revelation and special revelation work together in evangelism.
5. Pray for someone who has had limited gospel exposure, that creation would prepare their heart for the message.$gs$)
