-- Book 1 · Lesson 3.2: The Bondage of the Will to Sin
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('aa87d64e-f078-577d-a7e8-3167f754f112', 'e7942d9b-a207-536f-b703-1f1ec1e63209', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Jesus’s specific description — sin producing genuine “slavery,” not merely occasional misstep — reveal that the lost condition involves a genuine bondage of the will, not simply isolated, correctable choices a person could freely reverse through effort alone?

Jesus’s specific description reveals genuine bondage, since sin producing slavery, not occasional misstep, establishes this comprehensive captivity, meaning the lost condition involves this genuine bondage of the will, not merely isolated, correctable choices, but this passage’s specific description particularly requiring recognition of sin’s enslaving power as characteristic of the actual, comprehensive condition every person experiences apart from Christ’s own, liberating work and grace.$gs$),
  ('5947e817-02a2-5f08-b93f-7129d74680cd', 'e7942d9b-a207-536f-b703-1f1ec1e63209', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does recognizing this bondage explain why willpower alone, however genuinely sincere, consistently fails to produce lasting freedom, since a slave cannot free themselves through mere determination without an external, liberating rescue?

Recognizing this bondage explains willpower’s consistent failure, since a slave cannot self-liberate through mere determination, meaning sincere willpower alone consistently fails to produce lasting freedom, but this passage’s specific teaching requiring recognition that only Christ’s own, external, liberating work can genuinely free a person from this comprehensive bondage that sin’s actual, enslaving power produces throughout the human condition.$gs$),
  ('b21dcb35-329e-5543-b7b0-5629c30b6820', 'e7942d9b-a207-536f-b703-1f1ec1e63209', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Recognizing sin’s bondage does not mean people bear no genuine responsibility for their choices; Scripture holds both truths together — genuine bondage and genuine responsibility — without treating either as canceling the other.$gs$),
  ('cb0fd533-ae3e-5fcb-be81-9a8af27a4168', 'e7942d9b-a207-536f-b703-1f1ec1e63209', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read John 8:31-36 and reflect on sin’s genuine bondage and Christ’s promised, liberating freedom.
2. Identify an area where you once experienced this kind of bondage before genuine freedom in Christ.
3. Write about the difference between willpower-based change and Christ’s liberating, transformative work.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they explain this bondage to someone trapped in destructive patterns.
5. Pray for someone currently caught in this kind of bondage, that they would find genuine freedom in Christ.$gs$)
