-- Book 2 · Lesson 1.2: The Eternal Son
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('bb595902-f905-5736-b9bb-2aed0ecb0dff', 'ffd48adf-cc07-5271-be9c-72f8d8bd3e63', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Paul’s specific description — Christ existing “before all things” — reveal that the Son’s existence is genuinely eternal, not beginning at Bethlehem, but existing eternally alongside the Father before creation itself came into being?

Paul’s specific description reveals the Son’s genuinely eternal existence, since existing “before all things” establishes this pre-creation reality, meaning Christ’s existence did not begin at Bethlehem, but this passage’s specific description particularly requiring recognition of His genuine, eternal existence alongside the Father, not a created being with a beginning, throughout Scripture’s consistent testimony regarding the Son’s eternal, uncreated nature and being.$gs$),
  ('0f73fa83-1b2f-58bf-b72d-d197aeec1068', 'ffd48adf-cc07-5271-be9c-72f8d8bd3e63', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does this eternal existence provide important foundation for understanding the incarnation as the eternal Son genuinely entering time and taking on flesh, not a new being coming into existence, but this passage’s eternal Son adding genuine humanity to His already-existing, eternal person?

This eternal existence provides important foundation for understanding the incarnation, since the eternal Son entering time differs from a new being coming into existence, meaning the incarnation represents this eternal Son genuinely adding humanity, not beginning to exist, but this passage’s specific declaration particularly requiring this understanding throughout believers’ comprehension of the incarnation’s genuine, theological significance and meaning.$gs$),
  ('f8502d4c-82af-54af-b8dd-71329665321e', 'ffd48adf-cc07-5271-be9c-72f8d8bd3e63', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Affirming the Son’s eternal existence does not diminish the genuine reality of the incarnation; rather, it makes the incarnation more remarkable still — the eternal, uncreated Son genuinely, humbly entering the limitation of human, created existence.$gs$),
  ('59b59442-54f6-531c-a9b0-d4128a1d99a4', 'ffd48adf-cc07-5271-be9c-72f8d8bd3e63', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read Colossians 1:15-17 and John 1:1-3 and reflect on Christ’s genuine, eternal existence.
2. Write about the remarkable humility this eternal existence reveals regarding the incarnation.
3. Reflect on this truth in worship this week, considering the eternal Son’s humble entrance into creation.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how this truth shapes their own understanding of Christ’s person.
5. Journal about your own, deepened worship considering Christ’s eternal, yet humbly incarnate, existence.$gs$)
