-- Book 2 · Lesson 2.1: His Substitutionary Death
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('e8d89ed5-ef5e-54a2-af0d-5a4b1537666a', '80782e96-0ba7-5876-b29a-af325c90f779', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Peter’s specific language — Christ genuinely bearing “our sins,” not merely suffering alongside us — reveal that His death was an actual, substitutionary exchange, not merely an example of suffering or solidarity with human pain?

Peter’s specific language reveals an actual substitutionary exchange, since Christ genuinely bearing “our sins,” not merely suffering alongside, establishes this exchange, meaning His death represents this actual, substitutionary reality, not merely an example, but this passage’s specific language particularly requiring recognition of this genuine exchange — our sin, His bearing of it — as characteristic of Scripture’s consistent teaching regarding Christ’s actual, atoning death and its genuine significance for sinners.$gs$),
  ('987ef68f-d7a5-5738-9c6a-b9e180fb6303', '80782e96-0ba7-5876-b29a-af325c90f779', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does this substitutionary understanding provide the actual, specific content behind saying “Jesus died for me,” moving this from a vague, sentimental phrase into concrete, understood exchange that genuinely addresses the lost condition this plan’s companion study has explored?

This substitutionary understanding provides concrete content behind “Jesus died for me,” since concrete exchange differs from vague sentiment, meaning believers should understand this exchange specifically, not leaving the phrase vague, but grasping this passage’s concrete, substitutionary content as the actual, genuine reality behind Christ’s death addressing humanity’s genuine, lost condition throughout Scripture’s comprehensive teaching regarding atonement.$gs$),
  ('69c6672f-1d1a-52d6-afed-a9bef48e4253', '80782e96-0ba7-5876-b29a-af325c90f779', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Understanding Christ’s death as substitutionary does not mean this is the only genuine dimension of the atonement Scripture describes; substitution stands alongside other genuine truths — victory over evil, example of love — without any of these canceling or replacing the others.$gs$),
  ('6d051a65-0c38-5ab4-9bd8-d0c3a70faa8c', '80782e96-0ba7-5876-b29a-af325c90f779', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read 1 Peter 2:21-25 and reflect on Christ’s genuine, substitutionary bearing of sin.
2. Write a clear, concrete explanation of substitutionary atonement in your own words.
3. Practice explaining this concept out loud to your peer guide or a trusted friend.
4. Reflect specifically on a sin you find comfort knowing was genuinely borne by Christ.
5. Journal about your own, deepened gratitude for this concrete, costly exchange made on your behalf.$gs$)
