-- Book 2 · Lesson 2.2: His Bodily Resurrection
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('5ef99534-0c94-5518-b43c-92a4a3660e5a', 'ab7f90f7-bff8-54d2-9c04-c7114e4b658d', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Paul’s specific description — Christ as the “firstfruits” of resurrection — reveal that His resurrection was genuinely bodily and historical, establishing the actual pattern and guarantee for believers’ own, future resurrection rather than remaining merely symbolic or spiritual?

Paul’s specific description reveals genuinely bodily, historical resurrection, since “firstfruits” establishes an actual pattern guaranteeing a future harvest, meaning Christ’s resurrection was genuinely bodily and historical, not merely symbolic, but this passage’s specific description particularly requiring recognition of this bodily reality as establishing the actual, guaranteed pattern for believers’ own future resurrection throughout Scripture’s comprehensive teaching regarding this central, historical event.$gs$),
  ('9853442d-0bb2-55a2-a9c0-fffb0c2acb73', 'ab7f90f7-bff8-54d2-9c04-c7114e4b658d', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does this resurrection provide the actual, historical vindication that Christ’s claims and sacrifice were genuinely accepted by God, functioning as proof rather than mere postscript to the story told throughout Module 2’s exploration of His completed work?

This resurrection provides actual, historical vindication, since proof differs from mere postscript, meaning the resurrection functions as this passage’s genuine, historical proof that Christ’s claims and sacrifice were accepted by God, not remaining a mere addendum, but standing as necessary, evidential vindication throughout believers’ confident understanding of Christ’s completed, genuine work and accomplished salvation.$gs$),
  ('9cbed68e-442c-51b1-a64b-5277230777a1', 'ab7f90f7-bff8-54d2-9c04-c7114e4b658d', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Affirming the bodily resurrection does not mean the resurrected body was identical in every way to His pre-death, earthly body; Scripture describes a genuinely transformed, yet genuinely physical, body — the same pattern promised for believers’ own future resurrection.$gs$),
  ('9fa9a6aa-d515-505f-a4f1-69d181283bf3', 'ab7f90f7-bff8-54d2-9c04-c7114e4b658d', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read 1 Corinthians 15:12-23 and reflect on the resurrection as historical vindication and future pattern.
2. Write about why the resurrection functions as proof, not mere postscript, to the gospel story.
3. Reflect on the hope this provides for your own, future resurrection as a believer.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they explain the resurrection’s historical significance to skeptics.
5. Journal about your own confidence and hope, grounded in this historical, bodily resurrection.$gs$)
on conflict (id) do nothing;
