-- Book 2 · Lesson 3.1: Christ as Savior
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('ad979124-9e4b-5bc6-bcd9-db3d09fb79ab', 'f6cc095a-1c77-562b-8b83-8f0de9104fa3', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does this angelic announcement’s specific title — “Savior,” announced at Christ’s very birth — reveal that rescue, not merely instruction or example, was genuinely central to His mission from its earliest, announced beginning?

This angelic announcement’s specific title reveals rescue as genuinely central from the start, since “Savior,” announced at birth, establishes this mission’s core purpose from its earliest beginning, meaning rescue, not merely instruction, was genuinely central to Christ’s mission, but this passage’s specific title particularly requiring recognition of this saving purpose as central, not incidental, to understanding who Christ actually is and why He genuinely came throughout Scripture’s consistent testimony regarding His mission.$gs$),
  ('e88c25d5-c31c-56bf-9ce6-7b135768e9ff', 'f6cc095a-1c77-562b-8b83-8f0de9104fa3', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does embracing Christ specifically as Savior require believers to acknowledge their own, genuine need for rescue, since a savior is only meaningful to someone who recognizes they cannot save themselves, connecting directly to this plan’s earlier exploration of humanity’s lost condition?

Embracing Christ as Savior requires acknowledging genuine need for rescue, since a savior is only meaningful to someone recognizing their inability to self-save, meaning believers must genuinely acknowledge this need, not approaching Christ as merely helpful example, but recognizing this passage’s title as requiring honest acknowledgment of the lost condition this rescue specifically, genuinely addresses throughout believers’ understanding of who Christ actually is for them.$gs$),
  ('73c9e5d0-4479-5025-8420-44f6c40f9b8c', 'f6cc095a-1c77-562b-8b83-8f0de9104fa3', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Calling Christ “Savior” does not mean His role is limited to a single, past moment of rescue; His ongoing work of saving — sanctifying and preserving believers — continues throughout their entire, ongoing life and journey of faith.$gs$),
  ('682a9d18-5c41-50e3-8a7d-2a23f22d7b08', 'f6cc095a-1c77-562b-8b83-8f0de9104fa3', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read Luke 2:8-11 and reflect on “Savior” as the central, announced title of Christ’s mission.
2. Write about your own, genuine need for rescue that this title specifically addresses.
3. Identify a current area of life where you need to more genuinely entrust yourself to Christ as Savior.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they moved from viewing Christ as example to genuinely, personally as Savior.
5. Journal about your own, ongoing dependence on Christ’s continued, saving work throughout your life.$gs$)
