-- Book 3 · Lesson 1.3: The Reality of Our Helplessness
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('e04fe7de-eb92-564a-b67c-91ba4701305e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Main Scriptures$gs$, $gs$Psalm 49:7–9; Isaiah 64:6; John 6:44; Romans 3:19–24; Romans 7:18–25; Romans 8:7– 8; Ephesians 2:1–10; Titus 3:3–7.$gs$),
  ('41f9a9b3-b985-5b11-b7f5-77a552ab5acd', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 4, 'teaching', $gs$1. Humanity Cannot Save Itself$gs$, $gs$After understanding the reality of sin and judgment, we must face another important truth: human beings cannot save themselves.

People often believe that if they work hard enough, become good enough, or perform enough religious activities, they can make themselves acceptable to God.

They may believe that their good deeds can balance out their bad deeds. Others may think that if they improve their behavior, stop committing certain sins, or become more religious, God will accept them.

But the Bible teaches that salvation cannot be achieved through human effort.

The problem is not simply that people have made a few mistakes that can be corrected through better behavior. The problem is that sin has affected the human heart and placed humanity under guilt and judgment.

We need more than improvement. We need salvation.

A person who is drowning cannot rescue themselves merely by promising to become a better swimmer. They need someone to rescue them.

In the same way, humanity needs God to provide the salvation that we cannot accomplish ourselves.

This is one of the most important truths of the gospel. We must stop trusting in our own ability and recognize our need for Christ.$gs$),
  ('81d3349a-328c-5177-85f5-9806afe193ee', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 5, 'teaching', $gs$2. Good Works Cannot Remove the Guilt of Sin$gs$, $gs$Many people believe that good deeds can cancel out their wrongdoing.

They may think:

• "I have helped many people." • "I have given money to those in need." • "I have never committed a serious crime." • "I try to be kind to everyone." • "I attend religious services." • "I am better than many people I know."

These things may describe positive activities, but they cannot remove the guilt of sin before God.

Imagine someone who has committed a serious crime. They may later perform many good deeds, but those good deeds do not automatically erase the crime or remove their responsibility.

The same principle helps us understand our condition before God.

Good actions do not erase past rebellion. We cannot use good works as payment for the forgiveness we need.

Ephesians 2:8–9 teaches that salvation is by grace through faith and not by works, so that no one can boast.

This means that no person will be able to stand before God and claim that they earned eternal life through personal achievements.

Good works have an important place in the Christian life, but they are the result of salvation, not the price paid to obtain it.$gs$),
  ('cb6d44d5-7bff-5b03-a386-a9836ad4c569', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 6, 'teaching', $gs$3. Human Righteousness Cannot Meet God's Perfect Standard$gs$, $gs$Isaiah 64:6 describes human righteousness as inadequate before God's holiness.

This passage teaches that even our best efforts cannot make us perfectly righteous before God.

People may compare themselves with others and conclude that they are good. But God's standard is not based on whether we are better than another person.

His standard is perfect righteousness.

For example, a person may be more honest than a dishonest neighbor. But that does not mean the person has never lied, acted selfishly, or failed to love God completely.

Another person may be generous and respected in the community. Yet they may still struggle with pride, greed, anger, or unbelief.

The issue is not whether some people behave better than others in certain areas. The issue is whether anyone can claim to be perfectly righteous before God through their own efforts.

The Bible's answer is no.

This truth removes human boasting. We cannot use our achievements to demand salvation from God.

We must receive righteousness through the provision He has made in Jesus Christ.$gs$),
  ('d96cff62-c127-5f27-a53d-24fb3dec415a', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 7, 'teaching', $gs$4. The Human Heart Is Affected by Sin$gs$, $gs$The problem of human helplessness begins within us.

Sin has affected our thinking, desires, emotions, decisions, and understanding of God.

People do not naturally love God with the complete devotion He deserves. They often prefer their own desires and resist His authority.

Romans 8:7–8 teaches that the mind governed by the flesh is hostile toward God and does not submit to His law.

In this passage, the word flesh refers to human life under the influence and control of sin, not simply the physical body.

Paul is explaining that humanity's problem is deeper than a lack of information. People need a change that they cannot produce by themselves.

This is why simply telling someone to behave better cannot solve the problem of sin.

A person may understand that something is wrong and still struggle to turn away from it. They may know what is good but lack the spiritual ability and desire to live in full obedience to God.

The heart needs to be changed by God's grace.$gs$),
  ('9fc97232-425c-57ba-a861-8d84cacf3d35', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 8, 'teaching', $gs$5. People May Know What Is Right but Still Struggle to Do It$gs$, $gs$Romans 7:18–25 describes Paul's struggle with sin and the inability of human effort to produce the righteousness God requires.

Paul recognizes that he wants to do what is right but also experiences the power of sin working within him.

This passage shows the seriousness of the conflict between knowing God's standard and being unable to achieve righteousness through our own strength.

People often experience this conflict.

Someone may know that anger is destructive but continue responding with anger. Another person may know that dishonesty is wrong but continue lying when it seems beneficial. Someone may want to forgive but remain controlled by bitterness.

Knowing what is right is important, but knowledge alone cannot provide the power needed to overcome sin.

We need God's work within us.

This is why Christianity is not merely a system of moral instructions. The gospel announces that God gives new life through Christ and that the Holy Spirit works in believers to produce spiritual change.$gs$),
  ('bccd13a3-0087-5a54-b788-c34d423914c7', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 9, 'teaching', $gs$6. Religion Cannot Replace Salvation$gs$, $gs$One of the greatest dangers is believing that religious activity makes a person right with God.

A person may attend church regularly, know many Bible verses, participate in religious traditions, give money, and perform acts of service.

These activities can have value, but they cannot replace personal faith in Jesus Christ.

Jesus warned about people who appeared religious but did not truly know God.

In Matthew 23, He confronted religious leaders who focused heavily on outward religious practices while neglecting justice, mercy, and faithfulness.

Their problem was not that religious discipline was always wrong. Their problem was that outward activity had become a substitute for genuine devotion to God.

The same danger exists today.

People may believe that church attendance automatically makes them Christians. Others may trust in baptism, religious ceremonies, family tradition, or spiritual experiences without understanding the gospel.

Religious practices cannot forgive sin or create new spiritual life.

Only God can save, and He has provided salvation through Jesus Christ.$gs$),
  ('b03b4521-78c6-577b-ae90-4f28d8b51e73', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 10, 'teaching', $gs$7. No One Can Pay the Price for Their Own Salvation$gs$, $gs$Psalm 49:7–9 teaches that no person can redeem another person or pay the price needed to secure eternal life.

The passage reminds us that human wealth cannot purchase redemption.

Money can buy many things. It can provide food, housing, education, medical treatment, and comfort. But it cannot purchase forgiveness from God.

A person may be extremely wealthy and still be spiritually lost. Another person may have very little money but possess genuine faith in Christ.

The same is true of influence and social status. Human power cannot force God to forgive sin.

No human being can pay the full price required to remove their own guilt.

This is why the saving work of Jesus is essential. He did what humanity could not do. He gave His life as the sacrifice for sin and rose again.

Salvation is possible because God provided the price of redemption through His Son.$gs$),
  ('24ba537e-1fd7-5acc-8982-c5f55b23c9e2', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 11, 'teaching', $gs$8. We Are Spiritually Dead Apart From Christ$gs$, $gs$Ephesians 2:1–3 describes people as dead in trespasses and sins.

This is one of the strongest biblical descriptions of humanity's condition apart from Christ.

Spiritual death does not mean that people are physically dead or incapable of making ordinary decisions. People can work, build families, study, create, help others, and participate in society.

The passage is describing their spiritual condition before God.

Apart from God's saving work, people are separated from Him and unable to create spiritual life within themselves.

A physically dead person cannot bring themselves back to life through effort. In the same way, spiritually dead people need God to give them life.

This is why salvation is described as new birth and new creation.

John 3:3 teaches that a person must be born again to see the kingdom of God. Second Corinthians 5:17 describes someone who is in Christ as a new creation.

The gospel is not simply an invitation to improve our old life. It is an announcement that God gives new life through Jesus Christ.$gs$),
  ('dfcc1efb-9348-500f-be31-d6836ca161f5', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 12, 'teaching', $gs$9. We Depend on God's Grace From Beginning to End$gs$, $gs$Ephesians 2:4–10 gives a powerful contrast to the description of spiritual death in the first part of the chapter.

Paul begins by describing humanity's sinful condition, but then declares that God is rich in mercy and has great love for us.

Even when people were dead in their sins, God made them alive together with Christ.

This is the heart of grace.

Grace means that God gives kindness and salvation that we do not deserve and could never earn.

God does not save people because they have first made themselves worthy. He saves because of His mercy.

The passage also teaches that salvation is God's gift, not the result of human works.

But it does not stop there. Ephesians 2:10 explains that believers are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works.

This gives us an important order:

First, God saves us by grace.

Then, He begins changing our lives so that we can live in ways that honor Him.

Good works are important, but they come after salvation as its result.

We do not work our way into God's family. We are brought into His family through Christ, and then we learn to live as His people.$gs$),
  ('105f1a0a-2932-5629-ac62-a179a2e2831a', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 13, 'teaching', $gs$10. God Must Draw People to Christ$gs$, $gs$John 6:44 records Jesus teaching that no one can come to Him unless the Father draws them.

This teaches that salvation depends on God's initiative and grace.

Human beings do not independently discover a way to save themselves. God works through His Word and the Holy Spirit to reveal our need for Christ and bring us to faith.

This does not mean that people are treated like objects without responsibility. Scripture calls people to repent, believe, and come to Jesus. It also teaches that God is the One who works in the process of salvation.

We must therefore hold these biblical truths together.

We are responsible for our response to God's Word, and we are completely dependent on His grace for salvation.

This should produce humility and gratitude. No believer should boast as though they rescued themselves.$gs$),
  ('5b7f9a28-8199-57c4-9215-8117179ccc85', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 14, 'teaching', $gs$11. The Holy Spirit Gives New Life$gs$, $gs$The Holy Spirit is essential to the saving work of God.

A person cannot create spiritual life through willpower, education, or religious activity. The Holy Spirit works in the heart to bring about new birth and to begin transforming the believer.

Titus 3:3–7 describes humanity's former condition and then explains that God saved us not because of righteous things we had done, but because of His mercy. This salvation came through the washing of rebirth and renewal by the Holy Spirit.

The passage makes it clear that salvation is God's work.

The Holy Spirit brings new life, helps believers understand God's truth, strengthens them against sin, and produces spiritual fruit in their lives.

This does not mean that believers become perfect immediately. They continue to grow and must depend on the Spirit every day.

But the presence of the Holy Spirit means that salvation brings a real change in the believer's relationship with God.$gs$),
  ('b12cef0e-c5b0-51f7-ac35-72102dd5f44e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 15, 'teaching', $gs$12. Our Helplessness Makes Christ Our Only Hope$gs$, $gs$When we understand that we cannot save ourselves, we are prepared to appreciate Jesus Christ.

He is the One who lived the perfect life we could not live. He died for our sins, bore the judgment we deserved, and rose again.

He provides forgiveness that we cannot purchase, righteousness that we cannot achieve, and new life that we cannot create.

This is why the gospel is such good news.

If salvation depended on our ability to become good enough, no one could have confidence. We would always wonder whether we had done enough, whether our efforts were sufficient, or whether our failures had destroyed our hope.

But salvation rests on Christ's finished work.

We are called to repent and believe, placing our trust in Him rather than in ourselves.

The right response is not to become proud of our religious achievements. It is to come to Jesus with humility and receive the grace He offers.$gs$),
  ('c088bd4e-5290-5a6a-9536-afbe18838e8a', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 16, 'teaching', $gs$13. Understanding Our Helplessness Should Produce Humility and Compassion$gs$, $gs$When we understand our own need for grace, we should become more humble toward others.

We should never treat people who are struggling with sin as though we are naturally superior to them.

We were also sinners who needed God's mercy.

This does not mean that we approve of wrongdoing or ignore the harm sin causes. It means that we approach people with truth and compassion.

We should speak about sin honestly, but we should also explain the hope of salvation through Christ.

A person who understands grace should be willing to forgive, patient with people who are learning, and concerned about those who have not yet heard the gospel.

The truth that we cannot save ourselves should also make us more dependent on God in our daily Christian lives.

We needed His grace to begin the Christian life, and we continue to need His grace as we grow.$gs$),
  ('ff351f96-1be1-56b6-82fe-20588610863e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 17, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does this passage’s specific timing — Christ dying “while we were still sinners,” before any change on our part — reveal humanity’s genuine helplessness to address the problem Module 1 has explored through its own, unaided effort?

This passage’s specific timing reveals genuine helplessness, since Christ dying before any change establishes this prior, unaided rescue, meaning humanity’s genuine helplessness required this passage’s timing — action taken while still sinners — not waiting for human effort, but this passage’s specific timing particularly requiring recognition of genuine helplessness as the honest, necessary conclusion to this module’s comprehensive exploration of humanity’s actual, lost condition.$gs$),
  ('ecf57d1f-bdb9-597b-9b5a-80f97c0d23fe', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 18, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does acknowledging genuine helplessness — rather than assuming partial human contribution toward salvation — prepare a person to receive the gospel’s good news as genuine grace, not a cooperative achievement shared between God and human effort?

Acknowledging genuine helplessness prepares reception of grace, not cooperative achievement, since genuine grace differs from partial human contribution, meaning believers should honestly acknowledge this helplessness, not assuming partial contribution, but preparing to receive this passage’s genuine grace as entirely God’s own, gracious initiative throughout their understanding of the gospel’s actual, complete message.$gs$),
  ('26671c3c-c234-5715-96d8-fe2a1f7dbd9e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 19, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Acknowledging helplessness to save ourselves does not mean human response — repentance and faith — is unnecessary; it means even this response is itself enabled by grace, not an independent human contribution earning or completing salvation.$gs$),
  ('44e94298-d0c9-56fe-b153-6e375f6ad153', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 21, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Summary$gs$, $gs$Humanity cannot save itself from sin or judgment. Good works, religious activities, wealth, education, and personal effort cannot remove guilt or create new spiritual life. Sin has affected the human heart, leaving people dependent on God's mercy. Through Jesus Christ, God provides forgiveness, righteousness, and new life. Salvation is a gift of grace, received through faith, and the Holy Spirit works within believers to bring about spiritual renewal.

Receive and value God's grace. Salvation is a gift that cannot be earned. Respond with gratitude, humility, and faith.

Show compassion to others. Remember that every person needs the mercy of God. Speak the truth about sin while also making the hope of Christ clear.$gs$),
  ('326fb29d-474b-5d9d-b42b-0b7048d32b03', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 22, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read Romans 5:6-8 and reflect on Christ’s action taken while humanity was still genuinely helpless.
2. Write about any subtle belief you have held that your own effort contributed to your salvation.
3. Pray, releasing any remaining reliance on self-effort and resting fully in grace.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they moved from self-reliance toward genuine, humble dependence on grace.
5. Journal about your own, deepened gratitude as you prepare to explore the gospel’s good news in Module 2.$gs$)
on conflict (id) do nothing;
update public.p2p_lesson_sections set section_order = 20 where id = 'd91a954f-1226-57fb-acf5-777963cc0d58' and section_order <> 20;
update public.p2p_lesson_sections set section_order = 23 where id = '57801819-9e1e-5ee9-8505-d4a04d76ce89' and section_order <> 23;
insert into public.p2p_reflection_questions (id, lesson_id, question, display_order) values
  ('06b4b8c0-97b6-505d-b0e4-462cdac83f73', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Have I subtly believed my own effort contributed something toward earning my salvation?$gs$, 4),
  ('e01aef32-cb52-53e3-b613-daa74c5038c1', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$How does acknowledging genuine helplessness deepen my appreciation for grace as entirely God’s initiative?$gs$, 5),
  ('2a7415b2-e51a-5ec3-b2b7-b069e8a01f44', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$What relief do I find in knowing I was rescued while still in my sin, not after cleaning myself up first?$gs$, 6),
  ('0abb4603-beb9-56c7-a838-430b956c4751', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$How would I explain this genuine helplessness to someone who believes they can partly earn their way to God?$gs$, 7),
  ('a2987935-b6b2-52a5-99ad-9f68e6de30e1', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$What gratitude does this honest acknowledgment produce as I move toward this plan’s exploration of the good news?$gs$, 8),
  ('a3734ba7-9a77-5f92-97d0-287553db3aa7', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Why can humanity not save itself?$gs$, 9),
  ('4c7f0d4d-eac6-507c-8dac-9499ebb0ab5e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Why can good works not remove the guilt of sin?$gs$, 10),
  ('2a82227b-3c03-544e-9ef2-26a50920cc15', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$What does Isaiah 64:6 teach about human righteousness?$gs$, 11),
  ('e53b9a64-30d6-5479-8d48-2c110a403c03', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$How has sin affected the human heart?$gs$, 12),
  ('37e43b35-8d92-55aa-80f4-2c79845976b3', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Why is knowing what is right not enough to produce a righteous life?$gs$, 13),
  ('f452d045-e37c-542d-a3b9-133770b1dd2d', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Why can religious activity not replace personal faith in Christ?$gs$, 14),
  ('11c34673-b844-540f-80e4-0bb276da4b78', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$What does it mean to be spiritually dead in sin?$gs$, 15),
  ('5370e180-7188-55a1-b7b3-789ce16b2612', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$How does Ephesians 2:4–10 describe God's response to humanity's helpless condition?$gs$, 16),
  ('31ef2bf6-e6d8-5d28-af60-10f6ec35e1e0', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Why is God's grace necessary from the beginning of salvation?$gs$, 17),
  ('663cfc62-a55f-56c3-babd-332d62f30dbc', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$What role does the Holy Spirit play in giving new life?$gs$, 18),
  ('b700b253-f7fe-5067-af30-5e1adf8421ab', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$How should understanding our helplessness affect the way we treat others?$gs$, 19),
  ('5ee17fc8-9cd1-513f-8cc1-ed5a9bd7af7d', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', $gs$Why is Jesus Christ our only hope of salvation?$gs$, 20)
on conflict (id) do nothing;
insert into public.p2p_lesson_blocks (id, lesson_id, block_type, content, order_index, is_required, is_submittable) values
  ('0b5c145e-f903-5221-8394-695b97d34f15', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Main Scriptures\n\nPsalm 49:7–9; Isaiah 64:6; John 6:44; Romans 3:19–24; Romans 7:18–25; Romans 8:7– 8; Ephesians 2:1–10; Titus 3:3–7."}$gs$::jsonb, 3, false, false),
  ('1a51ece0-b16f-551a-b5d9-3a2eb9a76ed1', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"1. Humanity Cannot Save Itself\n\nAfter understanding the reality of sin and judgment, we must face another important truth: human beings cannot save themselves.\n\nPeople often believe that if they work hard enough, become good enough, or perform enough religious activities, they can make themselves acceptable to God.\n\nThey may believe that their good deeds can balance out their bad deeds. Others may think that if they improve their behavior, stop committing certain sins, or become more religious, God will accept them.\n\nBut the Bible teaches that salvation cannot be achieved through human effort.\n\nThe problem is not simply that people have made a few mistakes that can be corrected through better behavior. The problem is that sin has affected the human heart and placed humanity under guilt and judgment.\n\nWe need more than improvement. We need salvation.\n\nA person who is drowning cannot rescue themselves merely by promising to become a better swimmer. They need someone to rescue them.\n\nIn the same way, humanity needs God to provide the salvation that we cannot accomplish ourselves.\n\nThis is one of the most important truths of the gospel. We must stop trusting in our own ability and recognize our need for Christ."}$gs$::jsonb, 4, false, false),
  ('a008deaa-a3b4-5422-ba14-a9fab7c32515', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"2. Good Works Cannot Remove the Guilt of Sin\n\nMany people believe that good deeds can cancel out their wrongdoing.\n\nThey may think:\n\n• \"I have helped many people.\" • \"I have given money to those in need.\" • \"I have never committed a serious crime.\" • \"I try to be kind to everyone.\" • \"I attend religious services.\" • \"I am better than many people I know.\"\n\nThese things may describe positive activities, but they cannot remove the guilt of sin before God.\n\nImagine someone who has committed a serious crime. They may later perform many good deeds, but those good deeds do not automatically erase the crime or remove their responsibility.\n\nThe same principle helps us understand our condition before God.\n\nGood actions do not erase past rebellion. We cannot use good works as payment for the forgiveness we need.\n\nEphesians 2:8–9 teaches that salvation is by grace through faith and not by works, so that no one can boast.\n\nThis means that no person will be able to stand before God and claim that they earned eternal life through personal achievements.\n\nGood works have an important place in the Christian life, but they are the result of salvation, not the price paid to obtain it."}$gs$::jsonb, 5, false, false),
  ('4c456f4c-e0ee-5dc7-88ff-993f45021ecb', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"3. Human Righteousness Cannot Meet God's Perfect Standard\n\nIsaiah 64:6 describes human righteousness as inadequate before God's holiness.\n\nThis passage teaches that even our best efforts cannot make us perfectly righteous before God.\n\nPeople may compare themselves with others and conclude that they are good. But God's standard is not based on whether we are better than another person.\n\nHis standard is perfect righteousness.\n\nFor example, a person may be more honest than a dishonest neighbor. But that does not mean the person has never lied, acted selfishly, or failed to love God completely.\n\nAnother person may be generous and respected in the community. Yet they may still struggle with pride, greed, anger, or unbelief.\n\nThe issue is not whether some people behave better than others in certain areas. The issue is whether anyone can claim to be perfectly righteous before God through their own efforts.\n\nThe Bible's answer is no.\n\nThis truth removes human boasting. We cannot use our achievements to demand salvation from God.\n\nWe must receive righteousness through the provision He has made in Jesus Christ."}$gs$::jsonb, 6, false, false),
  ('687288c0-51dd-5fd2-a389-beae8318e143', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"4. The Human Heart Is Affected by Sin\n\nThe problem of human helplessness begins within us.\n\nSin has affected our thinking, desires, emotions, decisions, and understanding of God.\n\nPeople do not naturally love God with the complete devotion He deserves. They often prefer their own desires and resist His authority.\n\nRomans 8:7–8 teaches that the mind governed by the flesh is hostile toward God and does not submit to His law.\n\nIn this passage, the word flesh refers to human life under the influence and control of sin, not simply the physical body.\n\nPaul is explaining that humanity's problem is deeper than a lack of information. People need a change that they cannot produce by themselves.\n\nThis is why simply telling someone to behave better cannot solve the problem of sin.\n\nA person may understand that something is wrong and still struggle to turn away from it. They may know what is good but lack the spiritual ability and desire to live in full obedience to God.\n\nThe heart needs to be changed by God's grace."}$gs$::jsonb, 7, false, false),
  ('53456460-8dcb-56f6-9ce1-173eb5cfe6b0', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"5. People May Know What Is Right but Still Struggle to Do It\n\nRomans 7:18–25 describes Paul's struggle with sin and the inability of human effort to produce the righteousness God requires.\n\nPaul recognizes that he wants to do what is right but also experiences the power of sin working within him.\n\nThis passage shows the seriousness of the conflict between knowing God's standard and being unable to achieve righteousness through our own strength.\n\nPeople often experience this conflict.\n\nSomeone may know that anger is destructive but continue responding with anger. Another person may know that dishonesty is wrong but continue lying when it seems beneficial. Someone may want to forgive but remain controlled by bitterness.\n\nKnowing what is right is important, but knowledge alone cannot provide the power needed to overcome sin.\n\nWe need God's work within us.\n\nThis is why Christianity is not merely a system of moral instructions. The gospel announces that God gives new life through Christ and that the Holy Spirit works in believers to produce spiritual change."}$gs$::jsonb, 8, false, false),
  ('77bd2f0a-3724-5c03-bb09-cbaed64e04b8', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"6. Religion Cannot Replace Salvation\n\nOne of the greatest dangers is believing that religious activity makes a person right with God.\n\nA person may attend church regularly, know many Bible verses, participate in religious traditions, give money, and perform acts of service.\n\nThese activities can have value, but they cannot replace personal faith in Jesus Christ.\n\nJesus warned about people who appeared religious but did not truly know God.\n\nIn Matthew 23, He confronted religious leaders who focused heavily on outward religious practices while neglecting justice, mercy, and faithfulness.\n\nTheir problem was not that religious discipline was always wrong. Their problem was that outward activity had become a substitute for genuine devotion to God.\n\nThe same danger exists today.\n\nPeople may believe that church attendance automatically makes them Christians. Others may trust in baptism, religious ceremonies, family tradition, or spiritual experiences without understanding the gospel.\n\nReligious practices cannot forgive sin or create new spiritual life.\n\nOnly God can save, and He has provided salvation through Jesus Christ."}$gs$::jsonb, 9, false, false),
  ('a199ca7e-ae53-5611-9f43-2a4285395f61', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"7. No One Can Pay the Price for Their Own Salvation\n\nPsalm 49:7–9 teaches that no person can redeem another person or pay the price needed to secure eternal life.\n\nThe passage reminds us that human wealth cannot purchase redemption.\n\nMoney can buy many things. It can provide food, housing, education, medical treatment, and comfort. But it cannot purchase forgiveness from God.\n\nA person may be extremely wealthy and still be spiritually lost. Another person may have very little money but possess genuine faith in Christ.\n\nThe same is true of influence and social status. Human power cannot force God to forgive sin.\n\nNo human being can pay the full price required to remove their own guilt.\n\nThis is why the saving work of Jesus is essential. He did what humanity could not do. He gave His life as the sacrifice for sin and rose again.\n\nSalvation is possible because God provided the price of redemption through His Son."}$gs$::jsonb, 10, false, false),
  ('8bfaa251-426a-557e-918c-a773285676f1', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"8. We Are Spiritually Dead Apart From Christ\n\nEphesians 2:1–3 describes people as dead in trespasses and sins.\n\nThis is one of the strongest biblical descriptions of humanity's condition apart from Christ.\n\nSpiritual death does not mean that people are physically dead or incapable of making ordinary decisions. People can work, build families, study, create, help others, and participate in society.\n\nThe passage is describing their spiritual condition before God.\n\nApart from God's saving work, people are separated from Him and unable to create spiritual life within themselves.\n\nA physically dead person cannot bring themselves back to life through effort. In the same way, spiritually dead people need God to give them life.\n\nThis is why salvation is described as new birth and new creation.\n\nJohn 3:3 teaches that a person must be born again to see the kingdom of God. Second Corinthians 5:17 describes someone who is in Christ as a new creation.\n\nThe gospel is not simply an invitation to improve our old life. It is an announcement that God gives new life through Jesus Christ."}$gs$::jsonb, 11, false, false),
  ('7551a6f3-4d10-5831-a044-6d9ab052ef40', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"9. We Depend on God's Grace From Beginning to End\n\nEphesians 2:4–10 gives a powerful contrast to the description of spiritual death in the first part of the chapter.\n\nPaul begins by describing humanity's sinful condition, but then declares that God is rich in mercy and has great love for us.\n\nEven when people were dead in their sins, God made them alive together with Christ.\n\nThis is the heart of grace.\n\nGrace means that God gives kindness and salvation that we do not deserve and could never earn.\n\nGod does not save people because they have first made themselves worthy. He saves because of His mercy.\n\nThe passage also teaches that salvation is God's gift, not the result of human works.\n\nBut it does not stop there. Ephesians 2:10 explains that believers are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works.\n\nThis gives us an important order:\n\nFirst, God saves us by grace.\n\nThen, He begins changing our lives so that we can live in ways that honor Him.\n\nGood works are important, but they come after salvation as its result.\n\nWe do not work our way into God's family. We are brought into His family through Christ, and then we learn to live as His people."}$gs$::jsonb, 12, false, false),
  ('33878504-95fd-59aa-9669-fb11606bed7e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"10. God Must Draw People to Christ\n\nJohn 6:44 records Jesus teaching that no one can come to Him unless the Father draws them.\n\nThis teaches that salvation depends on God's initiative and grace.\n\nHuman beings do not independently discover a way to save themselves. God works through His Word and the Holy Spirit to reveal our need for Christ and bring us to faith.\n\nThis does not mean that people are treated like objects without responsibility. Scripture calls people to repent, believe, and come to Jesus. It also teaches that God is the One who works in the process of salvation.\n\nWe must therefore hold these biblical truths together.\n\nWe are responsible for our response to God's Word, and we are completely dependent on His grace for salvation.\n\nThis should produce humility and gratitude. No believer should boast as though they rescued themselves."}$gs$::jsonb, 13, false, false),
  ('3c014e7e-0996-5f4b-97c1-95d9bf2f4e5f', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"11. The Holy Spirit Gives New Life\n\nThe Holy Spirit is essential to the saving work of God.\n\nA person cannot create spiritual life through willpower, education, or religious activity. The Holy Spirit works in the heart to bring about new birth and to begin transforming the believer.\n\nTitus 3:3–7 describes humanity's former condition and then explains that God saved us not because of righteous things we had done, but because of His mercy. This salvation came through the washing of rebirth and renewal by the Holy Spirit.\n\nThe passage makes it clear that salvation is God's work.\n\nThe Holy Spirit brings new life, helps believers understand God's truth, strengthens them against sin, and produces spiritual fruit in their lives.\n\nThis does not mean that believers become perfect immediately. They continue to grow and must depend on the Spirit every day.\n\nBut the presence of the Holy Spirit means that salvation brings a real change in the believer's relationship with God."}$gs$::jsonb, 14, false, false),
  ('2b977c48-770d-588e-9c3f-2be85e99fdac', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"12. Our Helplessness Makes Christ Our Only Hope\n\nWhen we understand that we cannot save ourselves, we are prepared to appreciate Jesus Christ.\n\nHe is the One who lived the perfect life we could not live. He died for our sins, bore the judgment we deserved, and rose again.\n\nHe provides forgiveness that we cannot purchase, righteousness that we cannot achieve, and new life that we cannot create.\n\nThis is why the gospel is such good news.\n\nIf salvation depended on our ability to become good enough, no one could have confidence. We would always wonder whether we had done enough, whether our efforts were sufficient, or whether our failures had destroyed our hope.\n\nBut salvation rests on Christ's finished work.\n\nWe are called to repent and believe, placing our trust in Him rather than in ourselves.\n\nThe right response is not to become proud of our religious achievements. It is to come to Jesus with humility and receive the grace He offers."}$gs$::jsonb, 15, false, false),
  ('20e099aa-60f0-550b-8f56-78a01b9921bd', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"13. Understanding Our Helplessness Should Produce Humility and Compassion\n\nWhen we understand our own need for grace, we should become more humble toward others.\n\nWe should never treat people who are struggling with sin as though we are naturally superior to them.\n\nWe were also sinners who needed God's mercy.\n\nThis does not mean that we approve of wrongdoing or ignore the harm sin causes. It means that we approach people with truth and compassion.\n\nWe should speak about sin honestly, but we should also explain the hope of salvation through Christ.\n\nA person who understands grace should be willing to forgive, patient with people who are learning, and concerned about those who have not yet heard the gospel.\n\nThe truth that we cannot save ourselves should also make us more dependent on God in our daily Christian lives.\n\nWe needed His grace to begin the Christian life, and we continue to need His grace as we grow."}$gs$::jsonb, 16, false, false),
  ('4f4ab66b-3dd2-575e-bc44-7bea094262f7', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 1\n\nWhy does this passage’s specific timing — Christ dying “while we were still sinners,” before any change on our part — reveal humanity’s genuine helplessness to address the problem Module 1 has explored through its own, unaided effort?\n\nThis passage’s specific timing reveals genuine helplessness, since Christ dying before any change establishes this prior, unaided rescue, meaning humanity’s genuine helplessness required this passage’s timing — action taken while still sinners — not waiting for human effort, but this passage’s specific timing particularly requiring recognition of genuine helplessness as the honest, necessary conclusion to this module’s comprehensive exploration of humanity’s actual, lost condition."}$gs$::jsonb, 17, false, false),
  ('ef1e20b6-ee05-5abe-b676-dc7f810d28b3', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 2\n\nHow does acknowledging genuine helplessness — rather than assuming partial human contribution toward salvation — prepare a person to receive the gospel’s good news as genuine grace, not a cooperative achievement shared between God and human effort?\n\nAcknowledging genuine helplessness prepares reception of grace, not cooperative achievement, since genuine grace differs from partial human contribution, meaning believers should honestly acknowledge this helplessness, not assuming partial contribution, but preparing to receive this passage’s genuine grace as entirely God’s own, gracious initiative throughout their understanding of the gospel’s actual, complete message."}$gs$::jsonb, 18, false, false),
  ('7b180356-1121-5391-bff4-c87d9eabdd67', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Important Distinction\n\nAcknowledging helplessness to save ourselves does not mean human response — repentance and faith — is unnecessary; it means even this response is itself enabled by grace, not an independent human contribution earning or completing salvation."}$gs$::jsonb, 19, false, false),
  ('7d9e2770-7b7c-50cf-9d53-09894d369d3b', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Summary\n\nHumanity cannot save itself from sin or judgment. Good works, religious activities, wealth, education, and personal effort cannot remove guilt or create new spiritual life. Sin has affected the human heart, leaving people dependent on God's mercy. Through Jesus Christ, God provides forgiveness, righteousness, and new life. Salvation is a gift of grace, received through faith, and the Holy Spirit works within believers to bring about spiritual renewal.\n\nReceive and value God's grace. Salvation is a gift that cannot be earned. Respond with gratitude, humility, and faith.\n\nShow compassion to others. Remember that every person needs the mercy of God. Speak the truth about sin while also making the hope of Christ clear."}$gs$::jsonb, 21, false, false),
  ('49165e97-0a84-5b33-9347-a6190305441f', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Assignment\n\n1. Read Romans 5:6-8 and reflect on Christ’s action taken while humanity was still genuinely helpless.\n2. Write about any subtle belief you have held that your own effort contributed to your salvation.\n3. Pray, releasing any remaining reliance on self-effort and resting fully in grace.\n4. Discuss with your peer guide how they moved from self-reliance toward genuine, humble dependence on grace.\n5. Journal about your own, deepened gratitude as you prepare to explore the gospel’s good news in Module 2."}$gs$::jsonb, 22, false, false),
  ('8eaea6b2-4306-523d-9144-10f8baa324c3', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Have I subtly believed my own effort contributed something toward earning my salvation?"}$gs$::jsonb, 27, true, true),
  ('0f64b826-6319-5e07-96da-157a65b2558a', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How does acknowledging genuine helplessness deepen my appreciation for grace as entirely God’s initiative?"}$gs$::jsonb, 28, true, true),
  ('999b71d9-5a46-5914-ba81-6cf7a95a47d7', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What relief do I find in knowing I was rescued while still in my sin, not after cleaning myself up first?"}$gs$::jsonb, 29, true, true),
  ('3c1c5f63-3707-5923-8782-3ee92c1bb646', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How would I explain this genuine helplessness to someone who believes they can partly earn their way to God?"}$gs$::jsonb, 30, true, true),
  ('4b74c041-c460-5560-9749-0de883bf866a', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What gratitude does this honest acknowledgment produce as I move toward this plan’s exploration of the good news?"}$gs$::jsonb, 31, true, true),
  ('8dc212a6-d0a7-59c3-8d4b-dda6c30003a7', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why can humanity not save itself?"}$gs$::jsonb, 32, true, true),
  ('9e9ee0cc-1b7c-5f12-acc0-1065387751b9', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why can good works not remove the guilt of sin?"}$gs$::jsonb, 33, true, true),
  ('3784adf7-92fb-50b2-8562-6ec864088a8f', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What does Isaiah 64:6 teach about human righteousness?"}$gs$::jsonb, 34, true, true),
  ('ecad6b53-8b84-548e-a8ee-e6a0250976f2', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How has sin affected the human heart?"}$gs$::jsonb, 35, true, true),
  ('ef805ffb-963e-5272-a6a7-6d0146dbc60f', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why is knowing what is right not enough to produce a righteous life?"}$gs$::jsonb, 36, true, true),
  ('03105e92-81d9-5249-8af5-3cb677a02b05', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why can religious activity not replace personal faith in Christ?"}$gs$::jsonb, 37, true, true),
  ('191bff06-0e0c-59b6-8574-76b5f4f8f0bb', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What does it mean to be spiritually dead in sin?"}$gs$::jsonb, 38, true, true),
  ('9c7718ff-54f8-5278-ad51-675ed9e0c82b', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How does Ephesians 2:4–10 describe God's response to humanity's helpless condition?"}$gs$::jsonb, 39, true, true),
  ('85030522-118b-5708-9e4b-dd705d391951', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why is God's grace necessary from the beginning of salvation?"}$gs$::jsonb, 40, true, true),
  ('1020c112-b4ea-5956-b6ef-3f549a86e3de', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What role does the Holy Spirit play in giving new life?"}$gs$::jsonb, 41, true, true),
  ('ea11d22c-3025-573e-8195-b250ea906ebd', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How should understanding our helplessness affect the way we treat others?"}$gs$::jsonb, 42, true, true),
  ('3162000d-e3c1-5d07-a584-923ded8c595e', '4e1ac2a0-b595-5509-a15f-dafef10ddde5', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why is Jesus Christ our only hope of salvation?"}$gs$::jsonb, 43, true, true)
on conflict (id) do nothing;
