update public.p2p_lessons set subtitle = $gs$Part 3 · Lesson 2.3$gs$ where id = '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b' and subtitle is distinct from $gs$Part 3 · Lesson 2.3$gs$;

-- Book 3 · Lesson 2.1: Christ’s Death for Our Sins
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('04de51f3-2498-5386-b112-fe5cc2674866', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 1, 'teaching', $gs$About this module$gs$, $gs$The gospel is the good news of what God has done through Jesus Christ to save sinners. The previous module explained the bad news: every person has sinned, every person stands accountable before God, and no person can rescue themselves through their own efforts. This module now turns to the good news that answers that problem.

God did not leave humanity without hope. In His love and mercy, He sent His Son, Jesus Christ, into the world. Jesus lived the perfect life that we have failed to live. He willingly gave His life on the cross, taking the punishment that sinners deserve. He was buried, and on the third day He rose bodily from the dead.

His death and resurrection are not simply events from history. They are the foundation of salvation. Through Christ, God offers forgiveness, peace with Himself, a new standing before Him, and eternal life. These blessings are not earned through good behaviour, religious activities, or personal achievement. They are received by grace through faith in Jesus Christ.

This module will help learners understand why Jesus had to die, what His resurrection accomplished, and why salvation is a gift that cannot be earned. The aim is not merely to know the facts of the gospel but to understand and trust the Saviour at the centre of it.$gs$),
  ('1c1217d5-f99b-5297-bdb3-a573e55bf715', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 2, 'teaching', $gs$Main Scripture references$gs$, $gs$• Isaiah 53:4–6
• Matthew 20:28
• John 1:29
• John 3:16–18
• Romans 3:21–26
• Romans 4:23–25
• Romans 5:6–11
• Romans 6:23
• 1 Corinthians 15:1–8
• 2 Corinthians 5:21
• Ephesians 2:1–10
• Galatians 2:16
• Titus 3:3–7
• 1 Peter 2:24
• 1 Peter 3:18$gs$),
  ('4d768b03-ca9d-573b-8d3a-f467452d16c5', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Module objectives$gs$, $gs$By the end of this module, learners should be able to:

1. Explain why Jesus Christ died for sinners.
2. Understand that Jesus died as our substitute, taking the punishment our sins deserved.
3. Explain why the bodily resurrection of Jesus is essential to the gospel.
4. Understand the relationship between Christ's resurrection and our justification.
5. Explain what grace means and why salvation cannot be earned.
6. Understand that good works are the result of salvation, not the price paid for it.
7. Clearly explain the good news of the gospel to another person.$gs$),
  ('21e2d28a-af1c-5f31-a8c2-1bd5af6dc70b', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 6, 'teaching', $gs$Main Scriptures$gs$, $gs$Isaiah 53:4–6; Matthew 20:28; Romans 3:23–26; Romans 5:6–11; 1 Corinthians 15:3; 2 Corinthians 5:21; 1 Peter 2:24; 1 Peter 3:18.$gs$),
  ('722e2099-3a17-5aa1-80db-b08085d9d47f', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Introduction$gs$, $gs$The death of Jesus Christ is at the centre of the Christian gospel. Christianity does not teach that people can become right with God by trying harder, becoming more religious, or correcting their behaviour. It teaches that sinners need a Saviour, and that God has provided that Saviour through the death of His Son.

Jesus did not die because He had committed a crime or because He had failed in His mission. He died willingly and purposefully. His death was part of God's plan to rescue people from sin and bring them back into a right relationship with Himself.

The cross shows two important truths about God. First, it shows how serious sin is. Sin is not a small mistake that can simply be ignored. It is rebellion against the holy God who created us. Second, the cross shows the greatness of God's love. Although humanity had turned away from Him, God provided a way for sinners to be forgiven.

To understand the gospel, we must understand what Jesus accomplished through His death.$gs$),
  ('4fae9251-bb40-548c-bc38-d37100bf11df', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 8, 'teaching', $gs$1. Jesus Died Because Sin Required Judgment$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Romans 3:23–26; Romans 6:23

The Bible teaches that all people have sinned. Sin is not limited to terrible crimes or public wrongdoing. It includes every thought, desire, word, and action that falls short of God's perfect character and commands.

God is holy and righteous. He always does what is right, and He cannot treat evil as though it does not matter. A just judge cannot simply declare a guilty person innocent without dealing with the wrongdoing. In the same way, God does not pretend that human sin is harmless.

Romans 6:23 says that the wages of sin is death. A wage is something earned or deserved. Sin brings death because it separates people from the God who is the source of life. It also brings the reality of judgment.

This creates a problem that no human being can solve. We cannot remove our past sins by promising to behave better in the future. We cannot undo the wrong we have already done. Even if someone begins living differently today, that does not erase yesterday's guilt.

This is why the death of Jesus was necessary. The gospel is not simply that God decided to overlook sin. It is that God dealt with sin through the sacrifice of His Son.$gs$),
  ('c667a779-4721-5bbd-8cc7-c2d362b9e63c', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 9, 'teaching', $gs$2. Jesus Died Willingly$gs$, $gs$Scripture: John 10:17–18; Matthew 26:36–39; Philippians 2:5–8

Jesus knew that His mission would lead to the cross. He repeatedly told His disciples that He would suffer, be killed, and rise again. His death was not an accident that took God by surprise.

In the garden of Gethsemane, Jesus experienced deep sorrow as He faced the suffering ahead of Him. He prayed to the Father, expressing the weight of what was coming, yet He submitted Himself to the Father's will.

Jesus was not forced into the cross against His will. He willingly gave Himself. His willingness reveals the depth of His love and His obedience to the Father.

John 10:18 records Jesus saying that no one takes His life from Him; He lays it down willingly. This does not mean the human actions of those who arrested and crucified Him were acceptable. They were responsible for their wrongdoing. But God was working through these events to accomplish His saving purpose.

The cross was therefore both a human act of injustice and the fulfilment of God's plan of salvation.$gs$),
  ('f0cd1be6-2c4c-5c56-8dea-1addfa134be5', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 10, 'teaching', $gs$3. Jesus Died in the Place of Sinners$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Isaiah 53:4–6; 2 Corinthians 5:21; 1 Peter 3:18

One of the most important truths about the cross is that Jesus died as our substitute.

A substitute takes the place of another person. When the Bible says Jesus died for our sins, it means that He died on behalf of sinners and bore what they could not bear for themselves.

Isaiah 53 describes the suffering servant who was wounded for our transgressions and crushed for our iniquities. The punishment that brought us peace was placed upon Him. The passage teaches that the suffering of God's servant was connected to the wrongdoing of others.

Jesus had no sin of His own for which He needed to die. He was perfectly righteous. Yet He took the place of guilty people.

Second Corinthians 5:21 teaches that God made the sinless Christ to be sin for us so that in Him we might become the righteousness of God. This does not mean Jesus became morally sinful. He remained holy and obedient. It means that He took responsibility for our sin in the saving work He accomplished.

The cross is therefore not merely an example of courage or love. It is an act of substitution. Jesus stood in the place of sinners so that those who trust in Him could be forgiven and accepted by God.$gs$),
  ('356c08d8-ec0d-50fe-ae2c-e715848a0366', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 11, 'teaching', $gs$4. Jesus Bore the Punishment Our Sin Deserved$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Romans 3:24–26; Galatians 3:13; 1 Peter 2:24

Sin brings guilt and judgment. At the cross, Jesus bore the punishment that sinners deserved.

This is sometimes described as penal substitution. The word penal relates to punishment, and substitution means taking another person's place. The teaching is that Jesus took the judgment due to sinners so that they could receive forgiveness.

This does not mean the Father was cruel to an unwilling Son. The Father and the Son were united in the purpose of salvation. Jesus willingly offered Himself, and the Father sent Him in love. The cross displays the united saving work of God.

We must not imagine that God simply ignored justice because He loved humanity. God's love and justice meet at the cross. Sin is taken seriously, and mercy is provided through the sacrifice of Christ.

Romans 3 explains that God is both just and the one who justifies those who have faith in Jesus. In other words, God does not become unjust when He forgives sinners. He provides a righteous basis for forgiveness through Christ's sacrifice.

This is why the cross is so important. It tells us that forgiveness is costly. We receive it freely, but Jesus paid the price through His suffering and death.$gs$),
  ('7212930b-6731-5575-9890-cc36ecca8da3', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 12, 'teaching', $gs$5. Jesus' Death Demonstrates God's Love$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Romans 5:6–8; John 3:16; 1 John 4:9–10

The cross is the greatest demonstration of God's love for sinners.

Romans 5:8 teaches that God demonstrates His love for us in that Christ died for us while we were still sinners. God did not wait for humanity to become good enough before sending His Son.

This is important because people often think they must first improve themselves before they can come to God. They may believe they need to become more moral, overcome every bad habit, or prove their sincerity before God will receive them.

But the gospel tells a different story. God acted in love toward people who were helpless and sinful. Christ did not die for people who had already made themselves worthy. He died for people who needed mercy.

This does not mean God approves of sin. His love does not make sin unimportant. Rather, His love provides the rescue that sinners desperately need.

The cross also teaches us how deeply God values reconciliation. He did not save humanity through a small gesture or a simple declaration. He gave His Son so that people could be brought back into fellowship with Him.$gs$),
  ('b26271df-fcea-5953-9557-1831f6fe5629', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 13, 'teaching', $gs$6. Jesus' Death Brings Forgiveness$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Ephesians 1:7; Colossians 1:13–14; Hebrews 9:22

Forgiveness is one of the central blessings of Christ's death.

To forgive means to release someone from the guilt and debt of wrongdoing. Human forgiveness can involve choosing not to hold another person's offence against them. God's forgiveness is deeper because it deals with our guilt before His holy judgment.

Through Christ's blood, believers receive redemption and the forgiveness of sins. Redemption means being set free through a price that has been paid.

Before salvation, sinners stand guilty before God. Through Christ, they can be forgiven. Their sins are not simply forgotten as though they never mattered. They are dealt with through the sacrifice of Jesus.

This forgiveness includes past sins, present sins, and future sins. It does not give believers permission to continue living carelessly. Instead, it gives them a new relationship with God and a new reason to turn away from sin.

A person who understands the cost of forgiveness should not treat sin lightly. The cross teaches us both how serious sin is and how great God's mercy is.$gs$),
  ('d267386b-d7a5-5c9e-9f1c-8befc94ff824', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 14, 'teaching', $gs$7. Jesus' Death Reconciles Us to God$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Romans 5:1, 10–11; Colossians 1:19–22; 1 Peter 3:18

Sin separates people from God. It damages our relationship with Him and places us under His judgment.

Reconciliation means the restoration of a broken relationship. Through the death of Christ, people who were once enemies of God can be brought near to Him.

Romans 5 explains that while we were enemies, we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son. This is an extraordinary statement. God does not merely offer people a better religious experience. He brings them into peace with Himself.

The purpose of salvation is not only to escape punishment. It is also to know God and live in relationship with Him.

First Peter 3:18 says that Christ suffered once for sins, the righteous for the unrighteous, that He might bring us to God. This gives us a clear picture of the purpose of the cross. Jesus died so that sinners could be brought back to the Father.

The Christian life begins with this restored relationship. We no longer need to approach God as condemned enemies. Through Christ, believers can come to Him as those who have received mercy.$gs$),
  ('a2f4551d-6fa3-52e4-87ea-990b8c916e06', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 15, 'teaching', $gs$8. Jesus' Sacrifice Was Sufficient$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Hebrews 9:24–28; Hebrews 10:10–14

The death of Jesus was not an incomplete payment that must be repeated or supplemented by human effort. His sacrifice was sufficient.

In the Old Testament, sacrifices were offered repeatedly. They pointed toward the need for sin to be dealt with, but they could not finally remove sin. The repeated sacrifices showed that a greater and complete sacrifice was still needed.

Jesus offered Himself once for all. His sacrifice does not need to be repeated because His work is sufficient.

This means that people do not need to add their own suffering, religious ceremonies, charitable actions, or personal achievements to make Christ's sacrifice effective. Nothing we do can improve what Jesus has already accomplished.

We should respond with gratitude, obedience, and worship, but we must never imagine that our actions complete the work of the cross.

When Jesus died, He accomplished the saving work the Father had given Him. The believer's confidence rests in Christ's finished work, not in personal performance.$gs$),
  ('c16d1f4e-74c0-5776-9179-21ca8b204983', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 16, 'teaching', $gs$9. The Cross Calls for a Personal Response$gs$, $gs$Scripture: Mark 8:34–35; Galatians 2:20; 1 Peter 2:24

The death of Jesus is not only something to understand intellectually. It calls every person to respond.

A person can know many facts about the crucifixion without personally trusting Christ. They may know that Jesus was arrested, beaten, crucified, and buried. They may even believe that these events happened. But saving faith involves trusting Jesus and depending on what He accomplished.

The cross also changes the way believers live. First Peter 2:24 connects Christ's bearing of our sins with our turning away from sin and living for righteousness.

A believer does not obey God to pay Jesus back. That would be impossible. Instead, obedience becomes a grateful response to the love and mercy already received.

The cross teaches us that we no longer belong to ourselves. We have been bought at a great price. Our lives should therefore be shaped by love for Christ, humility, holiness, and service to others.$gs$),
  ('e04be0d7-3635-5a78-add5-edfeba9e8d08', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 17, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Paul’s specific phrase — Christ dying “for our sins,” according to Scripture’s own, prior testimony — reveal that His death was both a genuine, substitutionary rescue and the fulfillment of God’s own, long-promised plan?

Paul’s specific phrase reveals both substitutionary rescue and prophetic fulfillment, since dying “for our sins according to the Scriptures” establishes both realities together, meaning Christ’s death represents this genuine, substitutionary rescue while also fulfilling God’s own, long-promised plan, but this passage’s specific phrase particularly requiring recognition of both dimensions as characteristic of the gospel’s genuine, comprehensive good news regarding Christ’s death.$gs$),
  ('c6f5b2f9-110b-59d4-828e-e6538b53fe72', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 18, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does recognizing this death as fulfilling ancient Scripture provide additional, historical confidence that this was no accident or afterthought, but God’s own, planned, and prophesied solution to humanity’s genuine condition?

Recognizing this fulfillment provides additional confidence, since planned fulfillment differs from accident, meaning this passage’s connection to Scripture provides genuine, historical confidence that Christ’s death represents God’s own, planned solution, not accident or afterthought, but this passage’s specific connection particularly strengthening believers’ confidence in the gospel’s genuine, comprehensive, and prophesied good news.$gs$),
  ('948a7874-abdd-59c0-83b6-97e4f3edab74', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 19, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Emphasizing Christ’s death “according to the Scriptures” does not mean every detail of prophecy was mechanically predetermined without genuine human choice; it means God’s sovereign plan and human responsibility worked together in this event, both genuinely true simultaneously.$gs$),
  ('47b840bd-3270-55c2-a1c7-c01e062da39f', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 21, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Summary$gs$, $gs$Jesus Christ died willingly for the sins of humanity. He was sinless, yet He took the place of guilty sinners and bore the judgment they deserved. Through His death, God provides forgiveness, redemption, and reconciliation. The cross demonstrates God's love while also showing the seriousness of sin. Christ's sacrifice is complete and sufficient. Salvation is received through faith in Him, not through human achievement.$gs$),
  ('95bb1a8b-bbaf-59f7-9d77-53bffc841508', 'd6f733d3-6d15-5591-ad2b-9364534ec539', 22, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read 1 Corinthians 15:3 and Isaiah 53 side by side, noting the specific connections.
2. Write about how this fulfillment strengthens your own confidence in the gospel’s historical truth.
3. Share this connection with someone this week as evidence supporting the gospel’s genuine reliability.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they use prophetic fulfillment in their own gospel conversations.
5. Journal about your own, growing confidence in God’s planned, comprehensive rescue through Christ.$gs$)
