  ('22875f6f-5c8c-5f3e-b2f0-d5436627e39c', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Main Scriptures\n\nRomans 3:21–28; Romans 6:23; Ephesians 2:1–10; Galatians 2:16; Titus 3:3–7; 2 Timothy 1:8–10."}$gs$::jsonb, 3, false, false),
  ('4748ae49-affe-590b-818f-f157dee6437d', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Introduction\n\nGrace is one of the most important words in the Christian gospel. It describes God's undeserved kindness toward people who cannot earn His favour.\n\nHuman beings naturally think in terms of earning and deserving. We work to receive wages. We study to receive qualifications. We may perform well in order to receive praise or recognition. In many areas of life, people receive rewards because they have earned them.\n\nBut salvation does not work that way.\n\nThe Bible teaches that sinners cannot earn forgiveness or eternal life. Our good works cannot remove our guilt, and our religious efforts cannot make us righteous before God. Salvation is God's gift, provided through Jesus Christ and received through faith.\n\nGrace means that God gives what we do not deserve. He forgives sinners, brings them into His family, and gives them eternal life because of His love and mercy.\n\nThis lesson explains what grace means, why salvation cannot be earned, how grace changes the way we live, and why good works are the result of salvation rather than its price."}$gs$::jsonb, 4, false, false),
  ('e68c3336-cd68-5517-9eb6-3e9872df7bc9', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"1. Grace Means God's Undeserved Kindness\n\nScripture: Ephesians 2:4–7; Titus 3:4–7\n\nGrace is God's kindness toward people who have no claim on His favour.\n\nWhen we receive something because we have earned it, we receive a reward. When we receive something that we have not earned, it is a gift. Grace belongs to the second category.\n\nGod does not save people because they have reached a level of goodness that makes them worthy. He saves because He is loving, merciful, and gracious.\n\nTitus 3 explains that God saved us not because of righteous things we had done, but because of His mercy. This means that salvation begins with God's character and action, not with our achievements.\n\nThis truth removes human pride. No believer can honestly say that they saved themselves or that God accepted them because they were better than other people.\n\nEvery believer stands before God as someone who has received mercy.\n\nGrace also gives hope to people who feel that their past is too shameful for God to forgive. The gospel does not say that people must first become worthy before they can come to Christ. It invites sinners to come to Him for the mercy they need."}$gs$::jsonb, 5, false, false),
  ('7c7107fc-da98-52f8-ab7a-92eb86f882a6', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"2. Salvation Is a Gift, Not a Wage\n\nScripture: Romans 6:23; Ephesians 2:8–9\n\nRomans 6:23 contrasts the wages of sin with the gift of God. Sin earns death, but God gives eternal life through Jesus Christ.\n\nThe difference between wages and a gift is important.\n\nA wage is something a person receives because of work performed. A gift is something given freely by another person. Salvation is not a wage God owes us because we have done enough good things.\n\nEphesians 2:8–9 teaches that we are saved by grace through faith, and that this salvation is not from ourselves. It is the gift of God, not the result of works, so that no one can boast.\n\nThis means that nobody can stand before God and claim that eternal life is their rightful payment. We have no basis for boasting because salvation is entirely dependent on God's grace.\n\nPeople may compare themselves with others and feel that they are more deserving because they have behaved better or lived more responsibly. But the gospel does not compare sinners to one another as the standard of acceptance. It compares everyone to God's holiness.\n\nAll people need grace. No one can earn salvation by being less sinful than someone else."}$gs$::jsonb, 6, false, false),
  ('f3bc28ac-b9fa-5bf1-9863-f866e05791fb', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"3. We Are Saved by Grace Through Faith\n\nScripture: Ephesians 2:8; Romans 3:22–26; Galatians 2:16\n\nGrace is the source of salvation, and faith is the means by which we receive it.\n\nFaith means trusting in Jesus Christ and depending on Him. It is not a payment we offer God. It is the way we receive what God has provided.\n\nImagine someone offering you a gift. You do not earn the gift by accepting it. You simply receive what the giver has freely offered. In a similar way, saving faith receives Christ and depends on His finished work.\n\nThis does not mean faith is unimportant. The Bible clearly calls people to believe in Jesus. But faith does not become a human achievement that earns salvation.\n\nThe strength of saving faith is not found in how impressive the believer feels. Its value is found in the Saviour whom faith trusts.\n\nA person may have weak feelings and still genuinely trust Christ. Another person may have strong religious emotions but place their confidence in their own goodness. What matters is the object of faith.\n\nWe are not saved because we have performed faith perfectly. We are saved because Jesus Christ is a perfect and sufficient Saviour."}$gs$::jsonb, 7, false, false),
  ('c8506382-9689-5223-aa0f-23d7167337d2', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"4. Good Works Cannot Earn Salvation\n\nScripture: Romans 3:20; Galatians 2:16; Ephesians 2:8–9\n\nGood works are important, but they cannot purchase salvation.\n\nPeople often believe that God will accept them because they have tried to be kind, honest, generous, or religious. They may think that their good actions will outweigh their bad actions when God judges them.\n\nBut the Bible teaches that nobody is justified by works of the law. The law shows us God's righteous standard and exposes our sin, but it cannot make us righteous.\n\nEven our best actions are not enough to remove our guilt. We cannot balance our wrongdoing by doing good things later. A person who has committed a serious offence cannot erase that offence simply by performing acts of kindness afterward.\n\nGood works are valuable in their proper place. They can help others, reflect God's character, and demonstrate love. But they cannot pay for sin or create a right standing before God.\n\nIf good works could save us, then the death of Christ would not have been necessary. The cross shows that our need was much greater than human effort could meet."}$gs$::jsonb, 8, false, false),
  ('351e4c05-4c58-52c1-8b17-a3486b0d357f', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"5. We Are Saved Because God Is Merciful\n\nScripture: Ephesians 2:4–5; Titus 3:4–7; Romans 5:6–8\n\nMercy and grace are closely related. Grace is God's undeserved kindness, while mercy is His compassion toward people in need and under judgment.\n\nEphesians 2 describes humanity as dead in sin, but then declares that God, because of His great love and mercy, made believers alive with Christ.\n\nNotice where the change begins. It begins with God.\n\nGod did not wait for sinners to rescue themselves. He acted when they were helpless. He provided Christ as the Saviour and gives new life to those who trust Him.\n\nThis means salvation is not a reward for people who have managed to improve themselves. It is rescue for people who need God.\n\nThe mercy of God should produce humility. We should never look down on others as though we are naturally better than they are. The only reason a believer has hope is because God has shown mercy.\n\nThis should also produce compassion. If we have received forgiveness, we should be willing to show patience and kindness to others."}$gs$::jsonb, 9, false, false),
  ('e3f46d02-ebec-53b8-b7d1-0669a8a6f029', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"6. Grace Does Not Give Permission to Continue in Sin\n\nScripture: Romans 6:1–14; Titus 2:11–14\n\nA common misunderstanding is that grace means sin no longer matters. Some may reason that if God forgives freely, then people can continue living however they want.\n\nPaul directly addresses this question in Romans 6. He asks whether believers should continue in sin so that grace may increase. His answer is clear: absolutely not.\n\nGrace does not make sin acceptable. Grace changes the relationship a person has with sin.\n\nBefore salvation, sin ruled over us. Through Christ, believers receive a new identity and are called to live for righteousness. They are no longer to treat sin as their master.\n\nTitus 2 teaches that God's grace trains believers to reject ungodliness and worldly desires and to live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives.\n\nThis is an important truth: grace not only forgives; it also teaches and changes us.\n\nA person who understands grace will not use it as an excuse to remain in destructive behaviour. Instead, they will see grace as the reason they can turn away from sin and begin living in a way that honours God.\n\nChristian obedience is not an attempt to purchase God's love. It is a response to the love already received."}$gs$::jsonb, 10, false, false),
  ('0cb79d5a-0bf1-5392-87e4-f88593660f11', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"7. Good Works Are the Result of Salvation\n\nScripture: Ephesians 2:10; James 2:14–18; Matthew 5:16\n\nEphesians 2:10 follows immediately after the statement that salvation is not earned by works. It teaches that believers are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works.\n\nThis order is important.\n\nWe are not saved by good works, but we are saved for good works.\n\nGood works are the fruit of a changed life. They do not cause salvation; they follow salvation.\n\nWhen a person trusts Christ, God begins a work of change in that person's life. The believer begins learning to love what God loves, reject what God hates, and treat others according to God's commands.\n\nJames teaches that genuine faith will show itself through actions. He is not teaching that people earn salvation through good behaviour. He is explaining that living faith is not empty or inactive.\n\nFor example, a person who claims to love God but continually refuses to show love to others should examine whether their profession of faith is genuine. A living relationship with Christ should begin to produce visible changes.\n\nThese changes may take time. Believers grow at different rates, and no Christian becomes perfect in this life. But genuine salvation produces a new direction. The believer begins to live for God rather than treating personal desires as the highest authority."}$gs$::jsonb, 11, false, false),
  ('0e454c2a-9c8d-5f22-8200-3cba71dea05c', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"8. Grace Removes Boasting and Produces Humility\n\nScripture: Romans 3:27–28; 1 Corinthians 1:26–31; Ephesians 2:8–9\n\nIf salvation is a gift, there is no place for pride.\n\nA believer cannot boast about having earned God's acceptance. They cannot claim that their intelligence, discipline, religious background, or moral record made them worthy of salvation.\n\nEverything necessary for salvation has been provided through Christ.\n\nThis should change how Christians view themselves and other people. They should not think of themselves as spiritually superior. They should remember that they also needed mercy.\n\nIt should also affect the way Christians treat people who are struggling. Instead of responding with pride or harsh judgment, they should remember the patience God has shown them.\n\nGrace does not make us proud of our spiritual achievements. It makes us thankful for God's goodness.\n\nA church that understands grace should be a place where people are taught the truth, encouraged to grow, corrected when necessary, and reminded that salvation rests on Christ rather than human performance."}$gs$::jsonb, 12, false, false),
  ('13c871a8-0dec-5bf0-a636-d1a4af8a5bf6', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"9. Grace Gives Confidence in God's Saving Work\n\nScripture: Philippians 1:6; Romans 8:31–39; 2 Timothy 1:9–10\n\nBecause salvation begins with God's grace, believers can have confidence that God is committed to His saving purpose.\n\nPhilippians 1:6 expresses Paul's confidence that God, who began a good work in believers, will bring it to completion at the day of Christ Jesus.\n\nThis does not mean believers never struggle, fail, or need correction. The Christian life includes battles against sin, periods of weakness, and times of doubt. But God's grace remains the foundation of their hope.\n\nIf salvation depended on perfect human performance, no one could have lasting confidence. People would always wonder whether they had done enough.\n\nBut the gospel directs our confidence away from ourselves and toward Christ. He is the Saviour who died and rose again. His work is sufficient, and God's promises can be trusted.\n\nThis confidence should not make believers careless. It should encourage them to continue in faith, obedience, and dependence on God."}$gs$::jsonb, 13, false, false),
  ('25bb69fa-ddfc-52c6-8382-0617000e40a1', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"10. Grace Should Shape the Whole Christian Life\n\nScripture: Colossians 3:12–17; 1 Corinthians 15:10; Titus 2:11–14\n\nGrace is not only the way a person begins the Christian life. It is also the way believers continue living.\n\nSome Christians understand that they were saved by grace but later begin to think that God's love depends on how well they perform. They may become proud when they succeed or deeply discouraged when they fail.\n\nThe gospel reminds believers that their relationship with God rests on Christ. They should pursue holiness, but they must not confuse spiritual growth with earning acceptance.\n\nPaul describes how God's grace shaped his own life. In 1 Corinthians 15:10, he explains that he worked hard, yet he recognised that it was God's grace working with him.\n\nThis is the proper relationship between grace and effort. Christians should work faithfully, serve others, resist sin, and grow in obedience. But they do so because God has shown them grace and continues to strengthen them.\n\nGrace should therefore shape our worship, relationships, choices, service, and attitudes. It teaches us to live with gratitude rather than pride, obedience rather than carelessness, and confidence rather than fear."}$gs$::jsonb, 14, false, false),
  ('657c5908-627b-5cf2-a689-d0f61821f20e', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 1\n\nWhy does Paul’s specific description — salvation as genuine “gift,” not earned wage — reveal that grace represents the actual, defining character of the gospel’s good news, distinguishing it from every other religious system based on merit or earned favor?\n\nPaul’s specific description reveals grace as the gospel’s defining character, since salvation as genuine “gift,” not earned wage, establishes this distinguishing character, meaning grace represents the gospel’s actual, defining feature, distinguishing it from merit-based systems, but this passage’s specific description particularly requiring recognition of grace as characteristic of the gospel’s unique, comprehensive good news throughout believers’ understanding of salvation’s genuine, giftbased nature."}$gs$::jsonb, 15, false, false),
  ('94116f18-9632-547e-8c84-655f21f22df4', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 2\n\nHow does genuinely receiving this gift require humility — the same humility this module’s earlier lessons on helplessness prepared — since gifts, unlike wages, cannot be demanded or earned, only received with open, empty hands?\n\nGenuinely receiving this gift requires humility, since gifts cannot be demanded like wages, but received with open hands, meaning believers must approach salvation with this passage’s called-for humility, not demanding or earning, but receiving with the same empty-handed humility Module 1’s exploration of helplessness prepared, throughout their genuine, humble reception of this gift and good news."}$gs$::jsonb, 16, false, false),
  ('7f3d3c83-2db7-5267-9b1c-b80c4db2b3c3', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Important Distinction\n\nUnderstanding salvation as gift does not mean the response required — genuine faith — is itself a meritorious work earning salvation; even this faith is enabled by grace, a further gift, not an independent human achievement."}$gs$::jsonb, 17, false, false),
  ('a63ae1ac-a512-5ad1-b63a-b9860de6b78f', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Summary\n\nGrace is God's undeserved kindness toward sinners. Salvation is a gift received through faith in Jesus Christ, not a reward earned through good works. Jesus has provided everything necessary for our forgiveness and acceptance before God. Good works do not purchase salvation; they are the result of the new life God gives. Grace also teaches believers to reject sin, grow in holiness, serve others, and live with gratitude."}$gs$::jsonb, 19, false, false),
  ('458b6e14-bdd3-5ca6-b4b4-74f38b039e73', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Assignment\n\n1. Read Ephesians 2:8-9 and reflect on grace as genuine, unearned gift, not earned wage.\n2. Write about a time you struggled to simply receive a gift rather than trying to earn or repay it.\n3. Practice explaining grace clearly, distinguishing it from every merit-based religious system.\n4. Discuss with your peer guide how they have grown in genuinely resting in grace, not earned effort.\n5. Journal about your own, growing humility and gratitude as you receive this unearned, genuine gift."}$gs$::jsonb, 20, false, false),
  ('36e31b56-c94e-59ce-8b6c-cff9d4be41b7', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Have I approached salvation as something to be earned, rather than this passage’s genuine, freely given gift?"}$gs$::jsonb, 25, true, true),
  ('6de6d220-3398-54e1-9dd6-b308ecf23d9f', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How does understanding grace as gift, not wage, change how I approach my own standing before God?"}$gs$::jsonb, 26, true, true),
  ('1d212950-7eea-5c06-89f7-5c22e8b4613f', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What would it look like for me to receive this gift with genuine, open-handed humility this week?"}$gs$::jsonb, 27, true, true),
  ('19693f0c-68e7-568e-af69-9cbf6c27128c', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How would I explain grace clearly to someone who believes they must earn their way to God?"}$gs$::jsonb, 28, true, true),
  ('8bca3667-2560-5e2d-a3f4-0694cc74b27e', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What gratitude does this understanding of grace produce as you consider Module 3’s exploration of the gospel’s universal reach?"}$gs$::jsonb, 29, true, true),
  ('24345704-ddd6-5cc8-8681-ac404d3f6c6f', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What does the word grace mean?"}$gs$::jsonb, 30, true, true),
  ('7b11346b-b657-587d-9232-d98875635700', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why is salvation described as a gift rather than a wage?"}$gs$::jsonb, 31, true, true),
  ('c27583e5-4f90-5307-a605-b8b8db7e5c8e', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What is the difference between being saved by good works and being saved for good works?"}$gs$::jsonb, 32, true, true),
  ('a4257a0a-91cd-52de-b910-43d1d8356d12', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why can no person earn salvation?"}$gs$::jsonb, 33, true, true),
  ('3009e5cd-b27c-58d7-811f-743e30d7660b', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How does faith receive the salvation God provides?"}$gs$::jsonb, 34, true, true),
  ('d26fa8a2-927e-54dc-ae19-7deb354963a5', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Why does grace not give believers permission to continue living in sin?"}$gs$::jsonb, 35, true, true),
  ('c2c847c9-46c7-55a0-8fad-a1ee2c9bcf8c', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How should receiving grace affect the way you treat other people?"}$gs$::jsonb, 36, true, true),
  ('68eb3336-6350-5b3a-b4b6-d4da83e11ff8', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Are you relying on Christ's work or on your own religious and moral achievements?"}$gs$::jsonb, 37, true, true),
  ('831fb66c-a178-58f1-beb4-b4a1961da52f', '87b65ef6-88ef-556c-8f5b-c6997963aa3b', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What changes should God's grace produce in your daily life?"}$gs$::jsonb, 38, true, true)
