  ('52f79fa5-f7ec-5d9e-bfa5-e11805699121', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"3. The Gospel Changes Our Thinking\n\nScripture: Romans 12:1–2; Philippians 4:8\n\nThe way we live is strongly influenced by the way we think.\n\nIf our thinking is shaped mainly by selfish desires, cultural pressure, bitterness, pride, or fear, these things will eventually affect our actions.\n\nRomans 12 calls believers not to be conformed to the pattern of this world but to be transformed by the renewing of their minds.\n\nThis means that Christians must learn to think according to God's truth rather than simply accepting every idea that society presents.\n\nFor example, the world may teach that personal success is the highest goal. Scripture teaches that serving God and loving others are more important than gaining recognition.\n\nThe world may teach that revenge is the proper response to being hurt. Scripture teaches believers to reject bitterness and pursue forgiveness.\n\nThe world may teach that a person's value depends on appearance, wealth, or popularity. The gospel teaches that human worth is not measured by these things.\n\nRenewing the mind involves regularly reading Scripture, reflecting on what it teaches, rejecting false ideas, and learning to make decisions that honour God."}$gs$::jsonb, 7, false, false),
  ('d2ec4161-433d-580f-af41-5c44bf3ac154', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"4. The Gospel Changes How We Treat Others\n\nScripture: Ephesians 4:25–32; Colossians 3:12–14\n\nA gospel-shaped life should be visible in relationships.\n\nEphesians 4 teaches believers to speak truthfully, control anger, work honestly, share with those in need, and use words that build others up.\n\nIt also warns against bitterness, rage, slander, and harmful speech.\n\nThese instructions are practical. They show that the gospel should influence ordinary conversations, family relationships, friendships, and workplace behaviour.\n\nA person cannot claim to be deeply shaped by Christ while continually treating others with cruelty, dishonesty, and contempt without examining their life.\n\nColossians 3 encourages believers to put on compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience.\n\nThese qualities should not be limited to situations where people treat us well. Christian character becomes especially important when relationships are difficult.\n\nWhen we remember the mercy God has shown us, we have a reason to show mercy to others."}$gs$::jsonb, 8, false, false),
  ('9444eae2-893a-5ed0-8eaf-a2c404188481', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"5. The Gospel Changes Our Attitude Toward Work\n\nScripture: Colossians 3:22–24; Ephesians 6:5–8\n\nWork is one of the ordinary places where Christian character becomes visible.\n\nSome believers work in offices, factories, schools, shops, hospitals, homes, or other environments. Others may be unemployed, retired, studying, or caring for family members.\n\nWhatever our circumstances, the gospel should influence the way we approach our responsibilities.\n\nColossians 3 teaches believers to work sincerely, as people who are ultimately serving the Lord rather than merely trying to please human supervisors.\n\nThis does not mean accepting unfair treatment or refusing to address wrongdoing. It means working honestly and faithfully while remembering that God sees our actions.\n\nA Christian should not be dishonest simply because no one is watching. They should not treat colleagues badly or avoid responsibilities because they believe their work is unimportant.\n\nThe gospel teaches us that our daily responsibilities can become opportunities to honour God.\n\nFaithfulness in ordinary tasks is part of Christian discipleship."}$gs$::jsonb, 9, false, false),
  ('37275273-0e38-50a4-b888-84ceaac9a8c2', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"6. The Gospel Changes Our Use of Money and Possessions\n\nScripture: Luke 12:15; 2 Corinthians 8:9; 1 Timothy 6:6–10, 17–19\n\nMoney can easily become a source of pride, fear, greed, or false security.\n\nThe gospel reminds believers that their greatest treasure is not found in possessions but in Christ.\n\nJesus warned that life does not consist in the abundance of possessions. A person may have great wealth and still be spiritually poor.\n\nThe gospel also teaches generosity. In 2 Corinthians 8, Paul points to the grace of Christ, who became poor for our sake, as an example of generosity.\n\nBelievers should therefore handle money responsibly and use their resources in ways that honour God.\n\nThis includes providing for family responsibilities, avoiding dishonest gain, helping people in need, supporting gospel work, and refusing to make wealth the centre of life.\n\nA gospel-shaped attitude toward money is marked by gratitude and contentment rather than greed and constant comparison."}$gs$::jsonb, 10, false, false),
  ('f4d9c58e-b9d9-5237-930a-69f96a150cb5', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"7. The Gospel Changes How We Respond to Difficult People\n\nScripture: Romans 12:14–21; Matthew 5:43–48\n\nIt is easy to show kindness to people who treat us well. It is much more difficult when someone insults us, misunderstands us, or acts unfairly.\n\nThe gospel teaches believers to respond differently.\n\nRomans 12 tells Christians not to repay evil for evil. Instead, they are to seek peace where possible and overcome evil with good.\n\nThis does not mean accepting abuse or allowing dangerous behaviour to continue. It does not prevent people from seeking protection, reporting crimes, or establishing appropriate boundaries.\n\nBut it does challenge the desire for revenge and hatred.\n\nJesus taught His followers to love their enemies and pray for those who persecute them.\n\nThis kind of love reflects the mercy of God. We were not saved because we had always treated God rightly. We received mercy while we were sinners.\n\nA gospel-shaped person should therefore seek to respond to difficult people with truth, patience, and self-control."}$gs$::jsonb, 11, false, false),
  ('fb7426e2-5799-583a-b236-6d51a94d7c01', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"8. The Gospel Produces the Fruit of the Spirit\n\nScripture: Galatians 5:16–26\n\nThe Christian life is not only about avoiding wrongdoing. It is also about growing in godly character.\n\nGalatians 5 describes the fruit of the Spirit as love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control.\n\nThese qualities are the kind of character the Holy Spirit produces in the lives of believers.\n\nThe passage contrasts the fruit of the Spirit with the works of the flesh, which include sexual immorality, idolatry, hatred, jealousy, anger, selfish ambition, and other sinful behaviours.\n\nThis contrast shows that believers must not treat sinful desires as though they are harmless. They are called to walk by the Spirit.\n\nSpiritual growth takes time. A believer may struggle in one area while showing growth in another. But the direction of the Christian life should increasingly reflect the character of Christ.\n\nThe fruit of the Spirit is not something we produce through pride or self-effort alone. It grows as we depend on the Holy Spirit and follow God's Word."}$gs$::jsonb, 12, false, false),
  ('b3e9ebe7-ce18-5ecf-8b5d-8eeea497b0ed', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"9. The Gospel Should Be Visible in Our Families\n\nScripture: Ephesians 5:21–6:4; Colossians 3:18–21\n\nThe home is one of the clearest places where Christian character is tested.\n\nIt is possible for someone to appear kind and patient in public while behaving selfishly or harshly at home.\n\nThe gospel calls believers to live with love, patience, faithfulness, and responsibility in their families.\n\nEphesians 5 and 6 give instructions concerning marriage, children, and family relationships. These instructions emphasise love, respect, responsibility, and care.\n\nHusbands are called to love their wives sacrificially. Wives are called to respect their husbands. Parents are instructed not to provoke their children but to raise them in the Lord's teaching. Children are called to obey their parents in the Lord.\n\nThese instructions should be understood in the light of Christ's love and the responsibility believers have to honour God.\n\nThe gospel should make the home a place where people experience patience, forgiveness, honesty, and care.\n\nNo family is perfect, and every household experiences challenges. But believers should seek to allow the truth of Christ to shape how they speak, listen, correct, forgive, and support one another."}$gs$::jsonb, 13, false, false),
  ('dc177550-a59f-5752-bcf6-0aea6ecf70ee', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"10. The Gospel Makes Us People of Integrity\n\nScripture: Proverbs 10:9; Matthew 5:33–37; Titus 2:7–8\n\nIntegrity means being honest and consistent in our character.\n\nA person of integrity does not behave one way in church and another way when no one is watching. Their words and actions should agree.\n\nJesus taught His followers to speak truthfully and avoid dishonest promises. Titus encouraged believers to demonstrate integrity so that their lives would not bring unnecessary shame upon the teaching of God.\n\nIntegrity matters because people often judge the message we proclaim by the way we live.\n\nIf Christians regularly lie, cheat, manipulate, or break promises, their behaviour damages their witness.\n\nThis does not mean believers will never make mistakes. When they do, they should admit them, seek forgiveness, and make things right where possible.\n\nA gospel-shaped life is marked by honesty because believers serve a God who is truthful and faithful."}$gs$::jsonb, 14, false, false),
  ('257cd6e4-91fb-5d01-bfb9-175919def19a', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"11. The Gospel Should Influence Our Public Witness\n\nScripture: Matthew 5:13–16; Philippians 2:14–16; 1 Peter 2:11–12\n\nJesus described His followers as the salt of the earth and the light of the world.\n\nThese pictures show that Christian living should have a visible effect in the world.\n\nSalt was used to preserve and flavour food. Light makes things visible and helps people find their way. Jesus used these pictures to teach that His followers should live in ways that reflect God's character.\n\nBelievers should not hide their faith out of shame or fear. They should live honestly, serve others, and speak about Christ when appropriate opportunities arise.\n\nTheir lives should point people toward God rather than toward personal glory.\n\nThis does not mean trying to appear perfect or using good behaviour to manipulate people into becoming Christians. It means living sincerely according to the truth we believe.\n\nOur witness includes both what we say and how we live."}$gs$::jsonb, 15, false, false),
  ('0230c231-72e0-5690-a880-3bd516e27837', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"12. The Gospel Gives Us a Reason to Persevere\n\nScripture: Hebrews 12:1–3; Philippians 3:12–14\n\nLiving as a Christian is a lifelong journey.\n\nThere will be times of growth and times of struggle. Believers may experience discouragement, temptation, disappointment, or opposition.\n\nThe gospel gives us a reason to continue because our hope is in Jesus Christ.\n\nHebrews 12 encourages believers to run with perseverance, fixing their eyes on Jesus, who endured the cross and is now exalted.\n\nThis means that we should not measure the Christian life only by our immediate circumstances. We should keep looking to Christ and continue walking in faith.\n\nWhen we become discouraged, we should return to Scripture, pray, seek encouragement from other believers, and remember what Christ has done.\n\nA gospel-shaped life is not a life without difficulty. It is a life that continues to follow Jesus through every season."}$gs$::jsonb, 16, false, false),
  ('7708aaee-d2f9-5046-8b56-b8ae352c1d0d', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 1\n\nWhy does Paul’s specific instruction — conduct genuinely “worthy” of the gospel — reveal that believing the gospel rightly should produce a genuinely, observably different life, not remaining confined to merely intellectual agreement with its content?\n\nPaul’s specific instruction reveals that believing rightly produces observable difference, since conduct “worthy” of the gospel establishes this practical, observable expectation, meaning genuine gospel belief should produce this different life, not remaining confined to intellectual agreement, but this passage’s specific instruction particularly requiring recognition of gospel-shaped conduct as characteristic of authentic, comprehensive gospel faith throughout believers’ ongoing, daily life and observable witness."}$gs$::jsonb, 17, false, false),
  ('a462a782-2d9d-5e9f-8bc4-e8aa17722196', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 2\n\nHow does living as gospel-shaped people mean grace shapes both how believers treat their own failures — with humility, not despair — and how they treat others’ failures — with genuine mercy, not harsh judgment — reflecting the same grace this entire plan has explored?\n\nLiving as gospel-shaped people means grace shapes both self-treatment and treatment of others, since humility toward self and mercy toward others both reflect the same grace, meaning believers should extend this same, gospel-shaped grace both toward their own failures and toward others’, not harsh judgment in either direction, but reflecting this passage’s called-for, worthy conduct that genuinely, consistently displays the gospel’s comprehensive grace throughout their daily life and relationships."}$gs$::jsonb, 18, false, false),
  ('a0dbd1a4-abd2-568c-a486-3719ef914af4', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Important Distinction\n\nLiving worthy of the gospel does not mean achieving flawless, sinless conduct; it means allowing the gospel’s grace to genuinely, observably shape character over time, including humble, honest response when failure genuinely occurs."}$gs$::jsonb, 19, false, false),
  ('0c49d80a-4f02-5de2-af4a-77159b6db880', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Summary\n\nA gospel-shaped life is a life increasingly influenced by the death and resurrection of Jesus Christ. The love of Christ should direct our decisions, and God's grace should teach us to live in holiness. The gospel changes our thinking, relationships, work, use of money, family life, and public witness. It produces the fruit of the Spirit and calls us to persevere in faith. Our behaviour does not earn salvation, but it should increasingly reflect the new life we have received."}$gs$::jsonb, 21, false, false),
  ('48d2a94a-9940-51c6-975d-4d4e7ceefb29', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Assignment\n\n1. Read Philippians 1:27-30 and reflect on conduct genuinely worthy of the gospel.\n2. Identify one specific area of your conduct requiring greater alignment with the gospel you believe.\n3. Practice extending gospel-shaped grace toward either your own failure or someone else’s this week.\n4. Discuss with your peer guide how the gospel has practically, observably shaped their own character.\n5. Journal about your own, ongoing growth in living as a genuinely gospel-shaped person."}$gs$::jsonb, 22, false, false),
  ('50343a1c-ee5f-5dbe-9758-4fadfa65de16', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Does my daily conduct genuinely reflect the gospel I claim to believe, or remain disconnected from it?"}$gs$::jsonb, 27, true, true),
  ('606fe3c7-d5c8-54b5-af8d-ea025be4f736', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How does the gospel’s grace shape how I respond to my own failures — with humility, or with despair?"}$gs$::jsonb, 28, true, true),
  ('36503d7a-ae78-5da1-9c37-0c43788012ab', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How does this same grace shape how I treat others’ failures — with mercy, or with harsh judgment?"}$gs$::jsonb, 29, true, true),
  ('b8a26a42-445f-52af-a31d-17ff0916fd2b', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What specific area of my conduct requires greater alignment with the gospel I profess to believe?"}$gs$::jsonb, 30, true, true),
  ('0590cd76-27bb-5152-ae5d-109e4ffa353e', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How can I grow in allowing this gospel to genuinely, observably shape my character over time?"}$gs$::jsonb, 31, true, true),
  ('2dd5f31e-746d-5afa-9881-1d8fdad80d53', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How should the love of Christ influence the way you live?"}$gs$::jsonb, 32, true, true),
  ('c19d4e11-3fa9-506a-95d2-9f98b5498f90', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What does Titus 2 teach about the effect of grace on our behaviour?"}$gs$::jsonb, 33, true, true),
  ('80014a5e-293f-5f6a-b63e-0137be047213', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What does it mean to have your mind renewed by God's Word?"}$gs$::jsonb, 34, true, true),
  ('29db2a51-7587-5a21-abe0-3750091b0152', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How should the gospel affect your speech and relationships?"}$gs$::jsonb, 35, true, true),
  ('11df6dc3-4f25-5609-a037-08f04c21741d', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What does it mean to work as though you are serving the Lord?"}$gs$::jsonb, 36, true, true),
  ('d031c7df-3273-53a2-aff5-f9f1c5f2181c', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How should the gospel change your attitude toward money?"}$gs$::jsonb, 37, true, true),
  ('8324678e-b238-5c86-b503-e1f09563999f', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How can you respond to difficult people without seeking revenge?"}$gs$::jsonb, 38, true, true),
  ('30bd5211-34de-5257-a8f7-5525b1782471', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Which fruit of the Spirit do you most need to see growing in your life?"}$gs$::jsonb, 39, true, true),
  ('f5032b4c-1b80-5f83-befe-a1a3f37de790', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"How should the gospel influence the way you treat your family?"}$gs$::jsonb, 40, true, true),
  ('eac5cc36-d652-57e5-a731-29526db86361', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"Are your actions consistent with the Christian message you profess?"}$gs$::jsonb, 41, true, true),
  ('9bc0e920-15f9-530a-a63c-a9315134aba4', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What can you do to make your public witness more faithful?"}$gs$::jsonb, 42, true, true),
  ('e0dc9048-7d06-5485-9f58-b6dd168f94be', 'b50a7748-1088-5e8d-af40-9ce52893768a', 'reflection_question', $gs${"question":"What will help you continue following Christ when life becomes difficult?"}$gs$::jsonb, 43, true, true)
