-- Book 4 · Lesson 2.1: What Saving Faith Actually Is
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('25f39847-5e7d-5c26-a671-daf0ef4e039b', '0c3274c7-939c-5dac-baeb-f0d230bd52df', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does this passage’s specific description — faith as genuine “substance” and “evidence” — reveal that saving faith represents actual, substantial trust, not vague, wishful feeling disconnected from genuine confidence and reliance?

This passage’s specific description reveals faith as substantial trust, since “substance” and “evidence” establish this genuine, actual confidence, meaning saving faith represents this substantial trust, not vague, wishful feeling, but this passage’s specific description particularly requiring recognition of faith’s genuine, substantial nature as characteristic of authentic, biblical trust throughout believers’ actual, comprehensive response to the gospel’s specific claims and promises.$gs$),
  ('49e23745-b13a-5846-ae47-9d11e40cda03', '0c3274c7-939c-5dac-baeb-f0d230bd52df', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does saving faith specifically involve trusting in Christ’s completed work — not merely believing facts about Him — requiring genuine, personal reliance rather than intellectual agreement alone that stops short of actual, entrusted dependence?

Saving faith specifically involves trusting Christ’s completed work, since genuine, personal reliance differs from mere intellectual agreement, meaning saving faith requires this personal, entrusted dependence, not stopping at intellectual agreement alone, but pursuing this passage’s called-for, substantial trust as characteristic of genuine, saving faith throughout believers’ actual, personal reliance on Christ’s completed, sufficient work on their behalf.$gs$),
  ('b4131288-0e7e-525b-9f34-c2515752503d', '0c3274c7-939c-5dac-baeb-f0d230bd52df', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Genuine saving faith does not require the complete absence of doubt or difficult questions; it means placing genuine trust in what has become credible, even while continuing to work through remaining questions throughout ongoing growth and understanding.$gs$),
  ('9828894f-7677-5d50-a081-7caf950b69ba', '0c3274c7-939c-5dac-baeb-f0d230bd52df', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read Hebrews 11:1-6 and reflect on faith as genuine, substantial trust and confident reliance.
2. Write about the difference, in your own experience, between intellectual agreement and genuine trust.
3. Examine your own faith honestly, identifying whether it reflects genuine, personal reliance on Christ.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they distinguish genuine faith from mere intellectual agreement.
5. Journal about your own, growing understanding of what genuine, saving faith actually involves.$gs$)
