-- Book 5 · Lesson 4.1: Resting in Grace, Not Performance
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('ac72c7c7-25d7-5bd6-950e-d75cbff435c6', '73496f3d-0c65-5218-bf37-2cc94864ba9e', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Paul’s specific description — God Himself genuinely “working” within believers — reveal that living in confident assurance requires resting in grace’s ongoing power, not anxious, self-generated performance attempting to maintain or secure this assurance?

Paul’s specific description reveals assurance’s resting place in grace’s ongoing power, since God Himself genuinely “working” within believers establishes this gracious, ongoing power, meaning living in confident assurance requires this restful dependence, not anxious performance, but this passage’s specific description particularly requiring recognition of God’s ongoing, gracious work as characteristic of genuine, restful assurance throughout believers’ continued life and relationship with God’s sustaining, ongoing grace.$gs$),
  ('135d3436-e06a-51c9-ae67-227ce34a20d4', '73496f3d-0c65-5218-bf37-2cc94864ba9e', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does this restful dependence differ from passive indifference, since believers still genuinely participate — “work out” their salvation, as this passage’s broader context describes — while resting confidently in God’s own, ongoing, empowering work rather than anxious self-reliance?

This restful dependence differs from passive indifference, since genuine participation still occurs while resting in God’s power, not anxious self-reliance, meaning believers should hold both genuine participation and restful dependence together, not passive indifference, but pursuing this passage’s balanced, restful confidence throughout their ongoing, active yet genuinely restful life and relationship with God’s ongoing, empowering grace.$gs$),
  ('cdeaaec9-a645-5f0b-b8e5-14acc0c3ee71', '73496f3d-0c65-5218-bf37-2cc94864ba9e', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Resting in grace does not mean neglecting genuine spiritual disciplines or effort; it means this effort flows from restful confidence in God’s ongoing work, not anxious striving to maintain or earn an assurance already genuinely secured.$gs$),
  ('a3912a1f-bf65-51d1-8697-47414a1c7bca', '73496f3d-0c65-5218-bf37-2cc94864ba9e', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read Philippians 2:12-13 and reflect on God’s ongoing work empowering genuine, restful participation.
2. Identify a specific area of anxious, performance-based striving in your own spiritual life.
3. Release this anxiety this week, resting in God’s ongoing, empowering grace instead.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how they balance genuine effort with restful confidence in God’s work.
5. Journal about your own, growing rest in grace throughout your ongoing spiritual life and practice.$gs$)
