-- Book 7 · Lesson 3.1: Being Discipled by Someone Further Along
insert into public.p2p_lesson_sections (id, lesson_id, section_order, section_type, title, content) values
  ('b0ac7884-dbca-5daf-a0ce-d493aa2b4d1e', 'bf8d8cdd-9c1a-52fd-8aa4-9c996529155e', 3, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 1$gs$, $gs$Why does Paul’s specific address — genuinely calling Timothy his “son,” offering personal, ongoing guidance — reveal that genuine discipleship benefits significantly from relationship with someone further along in faith, not remaining confined to merely solitary, individual growth?

Paul’s specific address reveals discipleship’s benefit from mentoring relationship, since genuinely calling Timothy his “son” with ongoing guidance establishes this valuable, personal relationship, meaning genuine discipleship benefits significantly from this kind of relationship, not remaining confined to solitary growth, but this passage’s specific example particularly requiring recognition of mentoring relationship’s genuine value throughout believers’ ongoing growth and continued development as maturing disciples of Christ.$gs$),
  ('f20ae13b-db21-5e29-8869-1ed30c7bde2d', 'bf8d8cdd-9c1a-52fd-8aa4-9c996529155e', 4, 'teaching', $gs$Study question 2$gs$, $gs$How does actively seeking out this kind of mentoring relationship — someone genuinely further along, willing to walk personally alongside — provide practical guidance and encouragement that solitary reading and prayer, however genuinely valuable, cannot fully replace during ongoing growth?

Actively seeking mentoring provides practical guidance solitary practice cannot fully replace, since personal walking-alongside differs from solitary reading and prayer alone, meaning believers should actively seek this mentoring relationship, not relying exclusively on solitary practice, but pursuing this passage’s modeled, personal relationship throughout their ongoing, continued growth and development as maturing, growing disciples of Christ.$gs$),
  ('e5ea3259-40e5-5513-bacb-3bd69103a3a7', 'bf8d8cdd-9c1a-52fd-8aa4-9c996529155e', 5, 'teaching', $gs$Important Distinction$gs$, $gs$Seeking a mentor does not mean finding someone with flawless, complete answers to every question; it means finding someone genuinely further along who is willing to walk honestly alongside you, including through their own, continued imperfection and growth.$gs$),
  ('268e95aa-9a54-552e-ba3f-ae3817dda072', 'bf8d8cdd-9c1a-52fd-8aa4-9c996529155e', 7, 'teaching', $gs$Lesson Assignment$gs$, $gs$1. Read 2 Timothy 2:1-2 and reflect on Paul’s personal, ongoing investment in Timothy’s growth.
2. If you don’t have a mentor, take a concrete step this week toward seeking this kind of relationship.
3. Prepare two or three honest questions you would want to discuss with a mentor.
4. Discuss with your peer guide how mentoring relationships have shaped their own, continued growth.
5. Journal about your own, growing openness to this kind of personal, mentoring relationship.$gs$)
