  ('3279fa73-121a-5666-841a-b81125640a91', 'f810a9b6-eb5a-50d7-aba9-f4481ffd4fa6', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 1\n\nWhy does Paul’s specific instruction — Timothy committing truth to others who will “teach others also” — reveal that mature discipleship naturally moves toward investing in someone else’s growth, not remaining confined to merely receiving investment from others alone?\n\nPaul’s specific instruction reveals mature discipleship’s movement toward investing in others, since Timothy committing truth to others who will teach still others establishes this multiplying pattern, meaning mature discipleship naturally moves toward this investment, not remaining confined to merely receiving, but this passage’s specific instruction particularly requiring recognition of this multiplying pattern as characteristic of genuine, mature discipleship throughout believers’ continued growth and eventual investment in others’ own, developing faith and growth."}$gs$::jsonb, 3, false, false),
  ('3d7e401c-f34a-50b2-ab98-a3461caa10d9', 'f810a9b6-eb5a-50d7-aba9-f4481ffd4fa6', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Study question 2\n\nHow does this passage’s explicit, multi-generational vision — Paul to Timothy to faithful others to still others — reveal that genuine discipleship is meant to multiply across generations, requiring even relatively young believers to consider who they might begin investing in, even while still growing themselves?\n\nThis passage’s explicit, multigenerational vision reveals discipleship’s multiplying design, since this chain of investment extends across generations, meaning even relatively young believers should consider who they might invest in, not waiting for complete, personal maturity first, but pursuing this passage’s multi-generational vision throughout their own, continued growth and simultaneous investment in others’ developing faith and discipleship."}$gs$::jsonb, 4, false, false),
  ('8cad37bc-effa-5d0a-9fae-21addfa64c07', 'f810a9b6-eb5a-50d7-aba9-f4481ffd4fa6', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Important Distinction\n\nDiscipling someone behind you does not require having achieved flawless, complete spiritual maturity yourself; it means genuinely, honestly sharing what you have learned so far, even while continuing your own, ongoing growth and development as a maturing disciple."}$gs$::jsonb, 5, false, false),
  ('9e9d5b98-6c16-5c19-8464-b0988af40be0', 'f810a9b6-eb5a-50d7-aba9-f4481ffd4fa6', 'paragraph', $gs${"text":"Lesson Assignment\n\n1. Read 2 Timothy 2:1-2 once more and reflect on discipleship’s multiplying, multi-generational vision.\n2. Identify someone you could begin intentionally investing in, even at your own, current stage of growth.\n3. Take a step this month to begin this kind of intentional investment and relationship.\n4. Discuss with your peer guide how they have grown through investing in someone else’s discipleship.\n5. Journal about your own, growing commitment to this multiplying pattern of discipleship investment."}$gs$::jsonb, 7, false, false),
