update public.p2p_curriculums set admin_notes = $gs$Teaching approach (from "Pre-Salvation Curriculum — New Teaching Approach", migration 222):

PRE-SALVATION CORE CURRICULUM Revised Teaching Approach A simpler, clearer, learner-friendly format for all seven curriculum books Purpose To make every lesson easier to understand without weakening biblical truth. Key terms will be defined in ordinary language, explanations will be step-by-step, and reflection and assessment questions will match what has actually been taught. Revision framework and sample lesson 1. What Is Changing? The existing seven-book curriculum will retain its overall structure, biblical focus, and lesson subjects. The change is to how the material is taught: simpler wording, clearer explanations, practical examples, and questions that help learners demonstrate real understanding. The revised approach will: • Use everyday language. Avoid unnecessarily technical expressions and long, complicated sentences. • Define important terms. Introduce biblical and theological words, then explain each one plainly. • Explain ideas step by step. Do not assume learners already understand the subject. • Use Scripture carefully. Show how the Bible passage supports the point being taught. • Teach before testing. Reflection and assessment questions should only ask learners to work with ideas already explained. • Connect learning to life. Include practical examples and simple applications. 2. Standard Format for Every Lesson Section What it should contain 1. Lesson title and key ScripturKeeep the existing lesson title and identify the main Bible passage. 2. Definition Give a short, plain-language meaning of the main subject or key term. 3. Simple explanation Explain the teaching in clear steps, using short paragraphs. 4. Why this matters Show how the teaching connects to understanding salvation and following Christ. 5. Biblical example Use a relevant person, event, or passage from the Bible where it helps understanding. 6. Important things to understanCdlarify common misunderstandings and distinguish what the teaching does and does n 7. Personal reflection Ask a few direct, accessible questions that invite honest thought. 8. Comprehensive understandiTngescthreckall, comprehension, interpretation, and practical application. 9. Practical application Give one clear action, reading task, or short written response. 3. Improving Personal Reflections Reflection questions should help learners connect the lesson with their own understanding and everyday life. They should not be written like advanced theological examinations. Use questions such as: • What does this teaching mean in your own words? • What part of this lesson was new or surprising to you? • Have you ever thought about this subject differently? What has changed? • How could this truth affect the way you live? Avoid: vague questions, double- or triple-barrelled questions, and questions that require knowledge the lesson has not yet explained. 4. Improving Comprehensive Understanding Checks Checks should measure whether learners understand the lesson—not whether they can guess what the teacher wants to hear. Use a balanced mix of question types: • Definition: Can the learner explain the key term? • Comprehension: Can the learner describe the main teaching? • Bible understanding: Can the learner connect the teaching to the passage? • True or false: Can the learner identify a correct or incorrect statement and explain why? • Multiple choice: Can the learner distinguish between related ideas? • Application: Can the learner apply the teaching to a realistic situation? Answer guide: Provide a separate answer guide for teachers or facilitators. Answers should explain the reasoning, not merely give one-word responses. Where a question allows personal reflection, provide guidance rather than one rigid answer. 6. Applying the Approach Across All Seven Books 1. Pre-Salvation — Understanding humanity's condition before salvation. 2. Jesus Christ — Understanding who Jesus is and what He accomplished. 3. The Gospel — Understanding the good news of salvation. 4. Responding to the Gospel — Understanding repentance, faith, and confession. 5. Assurance of Salvation — Understanding the basis of assurance and how believers can know they belong to Christ. 6. Baptism and Identification with Christ — Understanding the meaning and importance of baptism. 7. From Convert to Disciple — Learning what it means to follow Jesus and grow as His disciple. 7. Editorial Standards for the Revision • Keep the existing seven-book sequence and lesson topics unless a genuine content issue requires discussion. • Retain relevant Bible references and explain their connection to the lesson. • Use short sentences, familiar words, and clear headings. • Explain theological terms at first use; do not remove important terms when learners need to know them. • Avoid repetition unless it reinforces an essential point. • Make reflection questions personal but not intrusive. • Make assessments fair, clear, and directly connected to the lesson. • Include a separate answer guide for objective questions and suggested teaching points for open-ended questions. • Ensure that simplification does not change the meaning of Scripture or present disputed interpretations as universally agreed. Intended result A learner with little or no theological background should be able to read a lesson, understand its main point, explain it in their own words, reflect on its meaning, and identify a practical response. The aim is clarity without loss of biblical depth.

Teaching note (sample lesson): This is a sample of the simpler style. The wording and level of detail should be adjusted to fit each lesson's actual Scripture passages and subject. The curriculum should preserve important biblical distinctions rather than oversimplifying them.$gs$ where id = 'f6a2b3f6-b66d-45d6-8065-c46d7b63441b' and admin_notes is null;

